= Game theory
{wiki}

Game theory studies decisions whose consequences depend on what other decision makers choose. A bidder's gain depends on competing bids in an <auction>; a driver's travel time depends on other drivers in a <congestion game>. This strategic interdependence makes choosing well a different problem from <optimization> against a fixed environment: each participant's <best response> depends on the others' choices.

A <normal-form game> specifies the players, their available <pure strategies>, and their <payoffs> for each <strategy profile>. Larger <payoffs> mean more preferred outcomes for that player. Unless stated otherwise, a two-player table below lists $(u_1,u_2)$: the row player's <payoff> first, the column player's second. Each player maximizes their own <expected utility>; the numbers need not represent money, and comparing different players' numerical scales is unnecessary.

The central question is whether everyone's choices fit together. A <Nash equilibrium> consists of mutual <best responses>: no <unilateral deviation> improves a player's <expected payoff>. It can involve definite choices, or <mixed strategies> that deliberately randomize. <Matching pennies> shows why randomization can be essential; the <coordination game> shows how players can share an interest in coordinating while disagreeing over which outcome to choose. Such shared gains are absent from a two-player <zero-sum game>, where one player's gain is exactly the other's loss.

These ideas are useful both for analyzing incentives and for designing them. <Mechanism design> asks how changing the rules changes participants' <best responses>; <evolutionary game theory> studies how successful strategies spread. The mathematical appeal is that local comparisons of <payoffs> can reveal a globally consistent pattern of choices. Consistency alone does not imply <Pareto efficiency>, uniqueness, or that actual play will converge to a <Nash equilibrium>.

John Nash's https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1063129/[1950 paper, Equilibrium Points in N-Person Games]{ref} established existence of a <mixed-strategy> <Nash equilibrium> for every <finite game>. His https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~klarson/teaching/F04-886/papers/nash51.pdf[1951 paper, Non-Cooperative Games, Annals of Mathematics 54, 286–295]{ref} developed the theory beyond two-player <zero-sum games>. The distinction between conflicting and shared interests is fundamental to the subject.

= Algorithmic game theory
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

= General-sum game
{parent=Game theory}

A <game> whose players' <utilities> are specified separately, without assuming a fixed sum. Improving one player's <utility> need not worsen another's. A finite <perfect-information game> can be solved by <backward induction> using entire <utility> tuples and each moving player's own coordinate, as in <max-n search>.

= Reachability game
{parent=Game theory}

A turn-based game on a <directed graph> in which a player tries to force a designated target set. Its winning region is a least <fixed point>: add that player's <vertices> with some winning successor and the opponent's <vertices> with all successors winning. The finite-<graph> version uses the same construction as a <winning-position attractor in an infinite game>.

With disjoint terminal wins for two players and infinite plays valued as draws, compute both reachability attractors while keeping terminals fixed. Entry ranks give terminating <winning strategies>; positions outside both regions admit defence avoiding a forced loss. A cycle alone is not a certificate of a draw if it also offers a forceable winning exit.

= Absent-minded decision problem
{parent=Game theory}

A decision problem in which the same <information set> can be reached more than once along a single path, so the decision-maker does not know which visit is occurring. It violates <perfect recall>. A <behavioural strategy> uses a common <probability> at each visit and independent random draws, making terminal probabilities nonlinear in that probability.

For instance, let stopping at the first visit pay zero, continuing and then stopping pay eight, and continuing twice pay one. With continuation probability $p$, <expected payoff> is $8p(1-p)+p^2=16/7-7(p-4/7)^2$. Its maximum $16/7$ exceeds the best deterministic-plan payoff of one. Randomizing once between the deterministic plans cannot reproduce the two fresh decisions.

= Behavioural strategy
{parent=Game theory}

= Behavioral strategy
{synonym}

An assignment of an action <probability distribution> to every <information set> of a player in an <extensive-form game>. Randomization is fresh at each encounter. It differs from a <mixed strategy>, which chooses a complete deterministic plan once before play. <Perfect recall> is central to equivalence of their induced outcome distributions.

= Mixed and behavioural security levels without perfect recall
{parent=Behavioural strategy}
{title2=$v_{\rm behavioural}=1/4,\quad v_{\rm mixed}=1/2$}

Consider a <zero-sum game> in which a player chooses a binary action, forgets it, and chooses again, while an opponent makes one hidden binary choice. The first player's <payoff> is one if all three choices agree and zero otherwise. The later <information set> merges both initial-action histories. This violates <perfect recall> without revisiting the same <information set>, unlike an <absent-minded decision problem>.

A <behavioural strategy> chooses the first and second actions with <independent> left <probabilities> $p,r$. Its <security level payoff> is $\min(pr,(1-p)(1-r))$, since the opponent selects the less favorable target action. This minimum is at most $\sqrt{p(1-p)r(1-r)}\le1/4$, attained at $p=r=1/2$.

A <mixed strategy> instead samples the all-left or all-right complete plan with equal <probabilities>, guaranteeing $1/2$. Against an opponent who chooses each target equally often, each complete constant plan earns $1/2$ and each nonconstant plan earns zero, so no <mixed strategy> can guarantee more. The separation comes from correlation between a sampled plan's actions; fresh <independent> randomizations cannot retain it after the initial action is forgotten.

= Perfect recall
{parent=Game theory}

A player has perfect recall when the <information sets> never erase that player's previously available information or earlier chosen actions. With perfect recall, randomizing once over complete <pure strategies> and using suitable independent randomizations at information sets can induce the same outcome distributions.

Without perfect recall, one <information set> may be encountered twice on the same play path. A <behavioural strategy> then uses the same probabilities with fresh draws at both encounters; its outcome distribution need not be attainable by any <mixed strategy> over deterministic plans.

= Kuhn's theorem
{c}
{parent=Perfect recall}

In a finite <extensive-form game> with <perfect recall>, each <mixed strategy> has a <realization-equivalent> <behavioural strategy>, and every <behavioural strategy> has a <realization-equivalent> <mixed strategy>. The latter direction samples all contingent actions independently in advance. For the former, condition the random complete plan on the player's own action and information history leading to an <information set>; <perfect recall> makes that own history common to its nodes. Conditional action <probabilities> then reproduce the terminal path factors. At an <information set> reached with zero probability under that player's own plan distribution, choose any action distribution. The result does not assert equivalence without <perfect recall>.<Extensive games and the problem of information by Harold W. Kuhn>[original article, pp. 193–216, perfect-recall equivalence]{ref}

= Perfect-information game
{parent=Game theory}

An <extensive-form game> in which each <information set> contains a single decision node. Each acting player knows the entire preceding sequence of moves. Finite games with this property can be solved for a <subgame perfect equilibrium> by <backward induction>.

= Information set
{parent=Game theory}

A collection of decision nodes that the acting player cannot distinguish when choosing an action in an <extensive-form game>. All nodes in an <information set> have the same acting player and available actions. A <behavioural strategy> assigns the same action probabilities throughout that set.

= Imperfect information
{parent=Information set}

In an <extensive-form game>, a player has imperfect information when some decision <information set> contains more than one node. The player cannot distinguish those histories at the time of acting. This does not itself imply failure of <perfect recall>: a player can remember all their own earlier information and actions while never having observed an opponent's hidden choice. Conversely, merging histories in which the player's own earlier actions differ violates <perfect recall>.

= Extensive-form game
{parent=Game theory}

A game described by a <game tree>, with the acting player and available actions specified at each decision node, <payoffs> at terminal nodes, and <information sets> indicating which decision nodes a player can distinguish. A <pure strategy> specifies an action at every information set belonging to that player, including ones not reached during realized play.

= Finite-horizon alternating-offer bargaining
{parent=Extensive-form game}

Two players alternate proposals for dividing a pie, with a fixed final round. Agreement ends the <game>; rejection shrinks the next available pie by a factor $0<\alpha<1$. Final rejection gives both players zero. Offers range continuously over the entire available pie. <Backward induction in games> determines immediate agreement and the proposer's share through the <continuous-offer bargaining acceptance lemma> and the <finite-horizon bargaining parity effect>. Discrete offer grids can change both the boundary behavior and the exact shares.

= Finite-horizon bargaining parity effect
{parent=Finite-horizon alternating-offer bargaining}
{title2=$P_k=\frac{1-(-\alpha)^k}{1+\alpha}$}

In <finite-horizon alternating-offer bargaining>, the proposer with $k$ rounds left retains fraction $P_k$ of the currently available pie. Terminal acceptance gives $P_1=1$. After rejection, the responder becomes proposer and can obtain fraction $\alpha P_{k-1}$ of the current pie. The <continuous-offer bargaining acceptance lemma> therefore gives $P_k=1-\alpha P_{k-1}$. Summing the resulting finite <geometric series> proves the displayed formula.

Odd horizons give shares above $1/(1+\alpha)$, decreasing toward it; even horizons give shares below it, increasing toward it. Between two available horizons $k_1<k_2$, choose $k_1$ if $k_1$ is odd and $k_2$ if $k_1$ is even. This concerns the initial proposer and immediate agreement, rather than waiting until the selected final round to obtain its share.

= Continuous-offer bargaining acceptance lemma
{parent=Finite-horizon alternating-offer bargaining}

Let a proposer divide a pie of size $M>0$ by continuously offering its responder $q\in[0,M]$. Rejection gives fixed continuation <payoffs> $b$ to the proposer and $c$ to the responder, with $0\leq c<M$ and $b<M-c$. Then a <subgame perfect equilibrium> must have the responder accept $q=c$ with <probability> one. The proposer offers exactly $c$, retaining $M-c$.

Sequential rationality forces rejection for $q<c$ and acceptance for $q>c$. If acceptance at $c$ had probability $a<1$, that offer would give the proposer $a(M-c)+(1-a)b<M-c$. Every larger offer gives less than $M-c$ and can be improved by a smaller offer still above $c$; every smaller offer gives $b<M-c$. The <supremum> $M-c$ would therefore be unattained. Randomizing offers cannot attain it either: the nonnegative gap from $M-c$ would be strictly positive almost surely and hence have positive <expectation>. No <best response> would exist, contradicting equilibrium. Acceptance at $c$ removes this gap and makes $q=c$ the unique optimal proposal. The assumptions $c<M$ and $b<M-c$ exclude degenerate indifference of the proposer.

= Backward induction in games
{parent=Extensive-form game}

= Zermelo's algorithm
{c}
{synonym}

In a finite <perfect-information game> without chance moves, solve terminal decision points first. At each earlier decision point, its player chooses an action maximizing its own <payoff> among the already solved continuations. Record a choice in every subgame, including those not reached by the root choice. This constructs a <subgame perfect equilibrium>. Tied choices may yield several equilibrium strategies and several outcomes. The name refers to <Ernst Zermelo>; the computational procedure is an application of <backward induction>.

= Unique outcome under strict outcome preferences
{parent=Backward induction in games}

Suppose a finite <perfect-information game> has no chance moves, and every player strictly ranks the possible outcome labels. Different terminal histories may have the same outcome label and thus the same <payoff>. Every subgame then has a unique <subgame perfect equilibrium> outcome, even though its equilibrium strategy need not be unique.

The proof is <backward induction>. Each child subgame has a unique continuation outcome. The moving player's strict ranking selects exactly one best label among those outcomes. Several actions can tie only by leading to that same label, which proves uniqueness at the current node. For a sequential election, the labels may be winning candidates: a voter can have several optimal ballots, all producing its same favourite achievable winner. If preferences have ties between different labels, the conclusion can fail.

= Realization-equivalent strategies
{parent=Extensive-form game}

= Realization-equivalent
{synonym}

Two strategies for the same player are realization-equivalent when they induce the same <probability distribution> on terminal histories against every fixed opposing <strategy profile>. The requirement is stronger than having equal <expected payoff> against one particular opponent. For a finite <extensive-form game> with <perfect recall>, <Kuhn's theorem> gives this equivalence between appropriate <mixed strategies> and <behavioural strategies>.

= Boolean game
{parent=Game theory}

A finite game represented succinctly by <propositional logic>. Each player controls a disjoint collection of <Boolean variables> and chooses their truth values. Together those choices determine a <Boolean valuation>. A player's goal is a <propositional formula>, with <payoff> one if satisfied and zero otherwise. The control sets normally form a <set partition> of all variables.

A <pure strategy> <strategy profile> is a <Nash equilibrium> if every unsatisfied player remains unsatisfied after every change to that player's controlled variables. Checking a candidate can therefore require solving a <Boolean satisfiability problem>, even though evaluating its current <payoffs> is easy.

= Candidate equilibrium checking in a Boolean game
{parent=Boolean game}
{title2=$\mathrm{coNP}\text{-complete}$}

Given a succinct <Boolean game> and a complete <pure strategy> <strategy profile>, determine whether it is a <Nash equilibrium>. The problem is <co-NP>-complete for goal <propositional formulas>, even with one player. This differs from <existential equilibrium checking in a Boolean game>, which searches for some qualifying <Nash equilibrium>.

A negative instance has a short <complexity certificate>: a currently unsatisfied player and new truth values for that player's controlled <Boolean variables> that make the goal true. Evaluating the current and changed <propositional formulas> takes <polynomial time>, proving membership in <co-NP>.

For hardness, given a <propositional formula> $F(X)$, let one player control $X$ and a fresh variable $z$, with goal $z\land F(X)$. The all-false candidate has <payoff> zero. It is a <Nash equilibrium> exactly when $F$ is an <unsatisfiable Boolean formula>, since any <satisfying assignment> permits an improvement by also setting $z$ true. This is a <polynomial-time many-one reduction> from unsatisfiability. The fresh guard ensures that the candidate loses independently of the input formula's value there.

= No-waste allocation
{parent=Game theory}

For nonnegative <additive valuations>, the requirement that each allocated item be assigned to an agent who values it strictly positively. This condition alone does not require allocating every item.

Let $S_i=\{j:v_{ij}>0\}$ and restrict agent $i$'s <constraint domain> to subsets of $S_i$, or to nonempty subsets when required. If $S_i\cap S_\ell=\varnothing$, the two bundles are automatically disjoint and each agent values the other's bundle at zero. Both directional <envy-free allocation> <binary constraints> are then automatic. Thus positive-support overlaps determine which pairs can still matter in the residual <constraint graph>.

= Envy-free allocation
{parent=Game theory}
{title2=$v_i(B_i)\ge v_i(B_\ell)\quad\text{for all }i,\ell$}

= Envy-freeness
{synonym}

An allocation of bundles $B_i$ is envy-free if each agent weakly prefers its own bundle to every other agent's bundle, evaluating both through its own value <function>. With an <additive valuation>,
$$
v_i(B_i)\ge v_i(B_\ell)
\iff
\sum_{j\in B_i}v_{ij}\ge\sum_{j\in B_\ell}v_{ij}.
$$
The reverse comparison uses $v_\ell$, so both directions are independent requirements. Ties are permitted. Empty bundles and unallocated items are separate modelling choices, not consequences of <envy-freeness>.

= Additive valuation
{parent=Game theory}
{title2=$v_i(S)=\sum_{j\in S}v_{ij}$}

A bundle-value <function> for which an agent's value for a <set> of items is the <sum> of its item values. Different agents may assign different values to the same item. With nonnegative item values, a bundle containing no positively valued item has value zero. The formula excludes interactions such as a pair of items being valuable only when held together.

= Fair division
{parent=Game theory}

Allocation of resources among agents according to a specified fairness requirement. For indivisible items, each bundle is a <set> of whole items, and a complete allocation assigns every item exactly once. An <envy-free allocation> is one fairness criterion. Existence depends on the permitted allocations: indivisibility can prevent fairness that would be possible after splitting resources or permitting unallocated items.

= Combinatorial game theory
{parent=Game theory}

= CGT
{synonym}
{c}

= Chomp
{parent=Combinatorial game theory}
{c}
{wiki}

An alternating grid-removal game in which choosing an occupied cell removes it and every occupied cell above and to its right. The player who removes the designated lower-left poison cell loses. Reachable occupied-cell <sets> are <order ideals> for coordinatewise order. A nonempty reachable position always contains the poison cell, and removing that cell empties the board. The <monotone boundary representation of a grid order ideal> supports compact board updates.

= Partizan game
{parent=Game theory}

= Partisan game
{synonym}

= Infinitesimal combinatorial game
{parent=Partizan game}

A game value lying strictly between $-r$ and $r$ for every positive <rational number> $r$, in the ordering of <partizan games>. Infinitesimal values can determine a sum whose numerical part is zero, while they cannot overturn a nonzero integer advantage.

= Star game
{parent=Infinitesimal combinatorial game}
{title2=$*$}

The <impartial game> $*=\{0\mid0\}$, so either player can move to zero. It is equivalent to a <Nim heap> of size one. It is incomparable with zero, and $*+*=0$ under the <disjunctive sum of games>.

= Up game
{parent=Infinitesimal combinatorial game}
{title2=$\uparrow$}

= Conway's up
{synonym}
{c}

= Hackenbush
{parent=Partizan game}
{c}

A <partizan game> played on a graph attached to the ground. Left removes blue edges and Right removes red edges; after a removal, all edges disconnected from the ground disappear. The player with no legal move loses under the <normal-play convention>.

= Hackenbush stalk
{parent=Hackenbush}
{c}

A <Hackenbush> position whose graph is a single path attached to the ground at one endpoint. Finite red-blue stalks have <dyadic rational> game values; the initial monochromatic run determines the integer part and subsequent colors determine successive binary fractions.

= Cold game
{parent=Partizan game}

= Number game
{parent=Partizan game}

A number game is a recursively defined normal-play <partizan game> whose options are number games and every left option is smaller than every right option. Its value is the simplest <surreal number> between those options. Finite number games have <dyadic rational> values.

= Pagoda function
{parent=Game theory}

= Game
{parent=Game theory}

= Tic-tac-toe
{parent=Game}
{wiki}

= Noughts and crosses
{synonym}

A two-player <game> on nine positions arranged in a three-by-three grid. Players alternate marking empty positions and stop when one has a full row, column or diagonal, or when the board is full. A game search must stop at the first win. Distinct move histories can reach the same position; canonical occupancy <sets> or <bitboards> make that equality available to <memoization>. Quotienting by board symmetry changes the requested output unless distinct oriented positions are subsequently restored.

= Tower of Hanoi
{parent=Game theory}
{c}

= Hanoi
{synonym}
{c}

= Stochastic game
{parent=Game theory}

= Game tree
{parent=Game theory}

= Max-n search
{parent=Game tree}
{title2=$\max^n$}

<Backward induction> with an $n$-component <utility> vector. At a node controlled by player $i$, evaluate each child, choose a child maximizing coordinate $i$ and copy its entire vector. A fixed tie rule makes the returned vector well defined. By <mathematical induction> from terminal positions, the selected continuation is a best response at every node of a finite <perfect-information game>, giving a <pure strategy> <subgame perfect equilibrium>. The vector must be copied intact; separate coordinatewise maxima need not describe any attainable outcome.

= Nearly-zero-sum pruning
{parent=Max-n search}
{title2=$\epsilon-y\le\alpha$}

Suppose every terminal pair satisfies $|U_A+U_B|\le\epsilon$. At a B-node with immediate A-parent, let $\alpha$ be the A-coordinate of a completed alternative already retained by the parent. If a completed B-child supplies B-coordinate $y$, then every eventual B-selected pair $(u,v)$ has $v\ge y$ and hence $u\le\epsilon-y$. Therefore $\epsilon-y<\alpha$ permits discarding this B-option and skipping its remaining children. Equality is also safe if the parent keeps its existing option on a tie.

The inequality remains valid at completed subtrees because <max-n search> copies one terminal pair. If the observed pair is $(x,y)$, then $x+y\ge-\epsilon$, so $u\le x+2\epsilon$ and $x+2\epsilon\le\alpha$ is a weaker sufficient test. At $\epsilon=0$ this reduces to the usual MIN-node <alpha-beta pruning> cutoff. A discarded option is not assigned a fabricated exact pair; the proof uses a certified alternative at the immediate parent.

= Chance node
{parent=Game tree}

A <game tree> node whose successor is chosen according to a specified <probability distribution>. Its <expected utility> is a weighted sum of successor values, rather than the maximum or minimum used at a player's decision node.

= Minimax search
{parent=Game tree}
{title2=$V(s)=\max_{s'}V(s')\ \text{or}\ \min_{s'}V(s')$}

<Backward induction> on a finite deterministic <game tree> of a two-player <zero-sum game>. Terminal <payoffs> are propagated by maximization at one player's decision nodes and minimization at the other's. With full information, the resulting choices give an optimal strategy against the opposing player's best response. A cyclic state <graph> requires a winning condition on infinite plays and a <graph> algorithm rather than unrestricted recursive unfolding.

= Alpha-beta pruning
{parent=Minimax search}

= Move ordering
{parent=Alpha-beta pruning}

The order in which legal child positions are searched. Good <move ordering> supplies useful alternatives early and increases <alpha-beta pruning>; poor <move ordering> can leave the worst-case <time complexity> of full <minimax search> unchanged.

= Normal-play convention
{parent=Game theory}

= Normal play
{synonym}

The convention that a player unable to move loses. In a finite <impartial game> without draws, a position is winning exactly when it has a move to a <losing position>.

= Impartial game
{parent=Game theory}

An impartial game is a <game> in which both players have the same legal moves from any given position. Under the <normal play convention>, a player with no legal move loses. For finite games without draws, <Grundy numbers> encode positions for the <Sprague-Grundy theorem>.

= Impartial normal-play game
{parent=Impartial game}

An <impartial game> played under the <normal-play convention>.

= Sprague-Grundy theory
{c}
{parent=Impartial game}

Sprague-Grundy theory studies finite acyclic <impartial games> under the <normal-play convention> by assigning each position a <Grundy number>. The assignment recursively takes the <minimum excludant> of the values of all legal followers. The <Sprague-Grundy theorem> then reduces a <disjunctive sum of games> to the <bitwise exclusive or> of its component values. This framework explains both why a zero value is losing and how independently understood games can be combined without exploring the entire product of their state spaces.

= Sprague-Grundy theorem
{c}
{parent=Sprague-Grundy theory}

Every finite acyclic <impartial game> under the <normal-play convention> has a <Grundy number>. The <Grundy number> of a sum of independent games is the bitwise <exclusive or> of their individual <Grundy numbers>, so the sum is losing exactly when that value is zero.

= Subtraction game
{parent=Impartial game}

A subtraction game has a heap of <nonnegative integer> size. A legal move subtracts an allowed positive amount without making the size negative; normal play declares an empty move set losing.

= Disjunctive sum of games
{parent=Impartial game}

= Disjunctive sums of games
{synonym}

= Disjunctive sum
{synonym}

In a disjunctive sum of games, each move changes exactly one component game. Under normal play, a player loses when no component has a legal move.

= Fibonacci Nim
{c}
{parent=Impartial game}

A heap game in which, after the first move, a player may remove at most twice the number removed on the preceding move. Under the usual first-move restriction of fewer than all counters, <Fibonacci numbers> and the <Zeckendorf representation> characterize the winning moves.

= Euclid game
{c}
{parent=Impartial game}

A game on two positive <integers> in which a move subtracts a positive multiple of the smaller from the larger without making it negative. The position is then reordered. This is an <impartial game> related to the <Euclidean algorithm>; the exact terminal and winning convention must be stated.

= Grundy number
{c}
{parent=Impartial game}
{title2=$g(P)$}

= Nimber
{synonym}

= Grundy value
{synonym}
{c}

= Sprague-Grundy value
{synonym}
{c}

The value $g(P)=\operatorname{mex}\{g(Q):P\to Q\}$ of a game position, where <mex> is the smallest nonnegative <integer> missing from the values of its legal successors. Terminal positions have value zero. The <Sprague-Grundy theorem> explains why these values combine by <exclusive or>.

= Nimber multiplication
{parent=Grundy number}

= Nim-product
{synonym}
{c}

= Nim
{c}
{parent=Impartial game}

An <impartial game> played with heaps of counters: a move removes a positive number of counters from exactly one heap. Under the <normal-play convention>, a position is losing exactly when the bitwise <exclusive or> of its heap sizes is zero.

= Nim heap
{parent=Nim}
{c}

= Nim-sum
{c}
{parent=Nim}

The bitwise <exclusive or> of a list of nonnegative <integers>. For heap sizes in ordinary <Nim>, a zero nim-sum characterizes a <losing position> under the <normal-play convention>.

= Losing position
{parent=Impartial game}

= P-position
{synonym}
{c}

= Losing state
{synonym}

= Prisoner's dilemma
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}
{title2=$T>R>P>S$}

Two people can each have a clear incentive to choose an outcome that leaves both worse off. The <prisoner's dilemma> isolates this tension between individual <best responses> and <Pareto efficiency>. For example, two partners can each save effort by withholding a promised contribution, yet both would prefer a successful joint project to mutual withholding. The model applies when each person's <payoffs> really have the ordering below; the names “cooperate” and “defect” alone do not establish a <prisoner's dilemma>.

In the symmetric two-player <normal-form game>, each player chooses cooperation $C$ or defection $D$ simultaneously, without observing the other's current choice. The four <payoffs> are the mutual-cooperation reward $R$, mutual-defection punishment $P$, sole-defector temptation $T$, and sole-cooperator <payoff> $S$, with $T>R>P>S$. Defection is a <strictly dominant strategy>: it beats cooperation against either opposing <pure strategy>. This definition and the resulting one-shot <Nash equilibrium> are discussed in https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/prisoner-dilemma/[Steven Kuhn, Prisoner's Dilemma, Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, section 1].

Take the concrete <payoffs> $T=5$, $R=3$, $P=1$, $S=0$. Each entry below gives the row player's <payoff> followed by the column player's <payoff>:
$$
\begin{array}{c|cc}
 & C & D\\\hline
C & (3,3) & (0,5)\\
D & (5,0) & (1,1)
\end{array}
$$

\Image[prisoners-dilemma-payoff-and-best-responses.png]
{title=Prisoner's dilemma: best-response arrows lead to mutual defection despite the gains from mutual cooperation}

For the row player, switching from $C$ to $D$ improves the <payoff> from $3$ to $5$ against $C$, and from $0$ to $1$ against $D$. The column player has the same comparisons, so $D$ is a <strictly dominant strategy> for each. Even against a <mixed strategy> choosing $C$ with <probability> $q$, the <expected payoff> advantage is $2q+(1-q)=1+q>0$. Thus cooperation cannot appear with positive <probability> in a <best response>, and $(D,D)$ is the unique one-shot <Nash equilibrium>, including <mixed strategies>.

At $(D,D)$, a <unilateral deviation> to $C$ lowers the deviator's <payoff> from $1$ to $0$. A joint change to $(C,C)$ instead raises both <payoffs> from $1$ to $3$: mutual cooperation Pareto dominates mutual defection, making the <Nash equilibrium> fail <Pareto efficiency>. A <Nash equilibrium> tests <unilateral deviations>, so it need not maximize shared gains. This is the useful lesson: improving the available outcome may require changing incentives, rather than telling each participant to choose a better <best response>.

The additional inequality $2R\ge T+S$ says that mutual cooperation gives each player at least as much average <payoff> as equal-time alternation between the two asymmetric outcomes. It is a further assumption, not a consequence of $T>R>P>S$. Here it holds strictly: $6>5$, and alternating exploitation gives each player average <payoff> $5/2<3$. This comparison concerns equal-time averages, not every discounted sequence. See https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/prisoner-dilemma/[Kuhn, section 3, on the additional cardinal-payoff condition].

= Donation game
{parent=Prisoner's dilemma}
{title2=$T=b,\ R=b-c,\ P=0,\ S=-c$}

A social game in which <cooperation> costs its actor $c$ and benefits its partner by $b$, with $b>c>0$. Mutual <cooperation> yields $b-c$ to each, unilateral defection yields $b$ to the defector and $-c$ to the cooperator, and mutual defection yields zero.

= Iterated prisoner's dilemma
{parent=Prisoner's dilemma}

The <iterated prisoner's dilemma> is a <repeated game> with a <prisoner's dilemma> at each round. Earlier choices can change later <best responses>: a present gain from defection may be outweighed by lost future cooperation. A <pure strategy> now specifies an action after every possible observed history, including histories that its own prescribed play would never reach. The horizon, observations, initial actions, and <payoff> convention must be specified before assessing a <Nash equilibrium> or <subgame perfect equilibrium>.

For the following worked example, repeat the <prisoner's dilemma> above infinitely at rounds $t=0,1,\ldots$. Assume public perfect monitoring: after each round both players observe both actions exactly, without error. They share a <discount factor> $0\le\delta<1$ and evaluate a path of stage <payoffs> $u_i(t)$ using the normalized discounted <payoff>
$$
U_i=(1-\delta)\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\delta^t u_i(t).
$$
The round-zero weight is one before normalization, including when $\delta=0$. A larger <discount factor> gives future <payoffs> more weight; the <geometric series> makes a constant stage <payoff> have the same normalized value. These assumptions and normalization follow https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-254-game-theory-with-engineering-applications-spring-2010/5a158a7558165d0e22b5b27fcfa01713_MIT6_254S10_lec15.pdf[Asu Ozdaglar, MIT 6.254, lecture 15, slides 6–15]{ref}. The numerical <payoffs> here are our $3,5,0,1$ example; the lecture uses a different table.

Suppose both players use <grim trigger>: play $C$ initially and while every past action of both players was $C$; after any observed $D$, play $D$ forever. With no <unilateral deviation>, each gets normalized <payoff> $3$. A first <unilateral deviation> to $D$ earns $5$ now, then at most $1$ each round because the opponent switches permanently to <always defect>. The deviator attains that continuation <payoff> by choosing $D$ thereafter. At any history of mutual cooperation, the comparison is therefore
$$
U_{\mathrm{cooperate}}=3,\qquad U_{\mathrm{deviate}}=(1-\delta)5+\delta\cdot1=5-4\delta.
$$
Cooperation is a <best response> precisely when
$$
3\ge 5-4\delta\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\delta\ge\frac12.
$$
Equivalently, without normalization the <geometric series> gives
$$
V_{\mathrm{cooperate}}=\frac{3}{1-\delta},\qquad V_{\mathrm{deviate}}=5+\frac{\delta}{1-\delta}.
$$
Multiplying both unnormalized <payoffs> by the same positive factor $1-\delta$ leaves the <best response> comparison unchanged.

\Image[repeated-prisoners-dilemma-discount-threshold.png]
{title=Repeated prisoner's dilemma: normalized cooperation and first-deviation payoffs cross at discount factor one half}

For example, with <discount factor> $\delta=1/4$, the first <unilateral deviation> gives normalized <payoff> $4>3$, so mutual <grim trigger> is not a <Nash equilibrium>. At $\delta=3/4$, it gives $2<3$, so cooperation is preferable. At $\delta=1/2$ the two <payoffs> tie: the incentive to cooperate is weak, which is sufficient for a <subgame perfect equilibrium>.

The punishment is credible after every history containing a defection: the opponent's <pure strategy> then prescribes $D$ forever, and $D$ remains the stage <best response>, earning $1$ rather than $0$. Together with the cooperative-history comparison, this establishes mutual <grim trigger> as a <subgame perfect equilibrium> for $1/2\le\delta<1$. Delaying the first <unilateral deviation> merely delays the same gain or loss; further cooperation during punishment only lowers the deviator's <payoff>. Mutual <always defect> also remains a <subgame perfect equilibrium>, so patience permits cooperation without making it inevitable.

This calculation assumes an infinite horizon and $\delta<1$; it does not define a discounted <payoff> at $\delta=1$. With a finite, commonly known final round and positive weight on each round, <backward induction> instead gives mutual defection in every round of this stage game. Observational errors change the model too: one mistaken defection permanently activates <grim trigger>. Historical dependence, initial memories, and the <payoff> time convention also affect whether a cooperative rule resists or invades other <pure strategies>; the existing <reactive strategy> and <Tit for tat> discussions concern those additional questions.

= Grim trigger
{parent=Iterated prisoner's dilemma}
{wiki}

= Grim trigger strategy
{synonym}

A <grim trigger> is a history-dependent <pure strategy> in a <repeated game> that switches permanently to punishment after a trigger event. In the <iterated prisoner's dilemma> above, the trigger is any player's observed defection, and punishment is <always defect>. Unlike <Tit for tat>, <grim trigger> never restores cooperation after the opponent cooperates again. Whether mutual <grim trigger> is a <subgame perfect equilibrium> depends on the stage <payoffs>, <discount factor>, and monitoring assumptions; it is not an unconditional property of the rule. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-254-game-theory-with-engineering-applications-spring-2010/5a158a7558165d0e22b5b27fcfa01713_MIT6_254S10_lec15.pdf#page=12[Ozdaglar, lecture 15, slides 12–15].

= Reactive strategy
{disambiguate=game theory}
{parent=Iterated prisoner's dilemma}
{title2=$(p,q)=(C_C,C_D)$}

= Reactive strategy
{synonym}

A <reactive strategy> cooperates with <probability> $p$ after the opponent cooperated and <probability> $q$ after the opponent defected. It ignores its own previous action. <Independent> behavioural randomization gives a four-state joint-action <Markov chain>. The reduced marginal recurrences depend on the prescribed initial action distribution.

= Always defect
{parent=Reactive strategy (game theory)}
{title2=$(0,0)$}

= ALLD
{c}
{synonym}

<Always defect> chooses defection independently of the opponent's actions.

= Always cooperate
{parent=Reactive strategy (game theory)}
{title2=$(1,1)$}

= ALLC
{c}
{synonym}

<Always cooperate> chooses cooperation independently of the opponent's actions.

= Tit for tat
{c}
{parent=Reactive strategy (game theory)}
{wiki}
{title2=$(1,0)$}

= TFT
{c}
{synonym}

<Tit for tat> cooperates initially and then repeats the opponent's previous action. Two such players cooperate forever without mistakes under this initial convention. It retaliates against defection and returns to cooperation after the opponent cooperates.

= Uniform payoff bound against Tit for tat
{parent=Tit for tat}
{title2=$\sum_{t<T}u_t\le RT+(T_{\rm tempt}-R)$}

In a symmetric two-action stage game write $R,P,T_{\rm tempt},S$ for mutual cooperation, mutual defection, sole defection and sole cooperation <payoffs>. Suppose $T_{\rm tempt}\ge R$, $P\le R$ and $T_{\rm tempt}+S\le2R$. Against an initially cooperative <Tit for tat>, every action <sequence> has limiting upper average <payoff> at most $R$.

For a deviator's actions $a_t$, put $a_{-1}=C$, $h(C)=0$ and $h(D)=T_{\rm tempt}-R$. The opponent at time $t$ plays $a_{t-1}$. Check the four transitions: $C$ to $C$ and $C$ to $D$ satisfy
$$
u(a_t,a_{t-1})\le R+h(a_t)-h(a_{t-1})
$$
with equality; $D$ to $C$ uses $S\le2R-T_{\rm tempt}$, and $D$ to $D$ uses $P\le R$. A <telescoping sum> gives the displayed finite-horizon bound. Divide by the horizon and let it increase. Mutual <Tit for tat> yields $R$ every round, so it is a <Nash equilibrium> under the <long-run average payoff> criterion. The argument is pathwise and therefore covers arbitrary deviations representable by <finite-state machines>, as well as action <sequences> outside that class.

= Tit for tat invasion of a reactive resident
{c}
{parent=Tit for tat}
{title2=$w_T-w_M=\epsilon[R-g(z)]$}

For a reactive resident $(p,q)$ with $|p-q|<1$, let $z=q/(1-p+q)$ and $g(z)=Rz^2+(S+T)z(1-z)+P(1-z)^2$. Its self-payoff and both ordered cross-payoffs against <Tit for tat> equal $g(z)$. At introduced <Tit for tat> frequency $\epsilon$, its fitness difference is therefore the displayed expression. If $2R\ge S+T$, it is positive for $z<1$ because $R-g(z)=(1-z)[R-P+(R+P-S-T)z]$. Fully cooperative reached histories tie; the deterministic opposite-response strategy needs a separate cycle calculation. Initial advantage is order frequency rather than a positive invasion exponent at zero.

= Finite-horizon invasion threshold of Tit for tat against unconditional defection
{parent=Tit for tat invasion of a reactive resident}
{title2=$\epsilon_c=(P-S)/[L(R-P)-(T+S-2P)]$}

In an $L$-round <iterated prisoner's dilemma>, <Tit for tat> against <always defect> gets $S$ once and $P$ thereafter; the defector gets $T$ once and $P$ thereafter. Their self-payoffs are $R,P$. At introduced frequency $\epsilon$, subtracting mixed-population <payoffs> gives $\epsilon[(R-P)-(T+S-2P)/L]-(P-S)/L$. The displayed positive threshold applies when its denominator is positive and the threshold is less than one. Taking $L\to\infty$ first removes this finite entry cost, so the horizon and rare-mutant <limits> cannot be casually interchanged.

= Neutrality between Tit for tat and unconditional cooperation
{parent=Tit for tat}
{title2=$e(T,T)=e(C,T)=e(T,C)=e(C,C)=R$}

Under cooperative initial memories, matches involving only <Tit for tat> and <always cooperate> contain only cooperation, so all four <payoffs> equal $R$. The <two-condition criterion for evolutionary stability> has both differences zero. Thus <Tit for tat> is not an <evolutionarily stable strategy> against a strategy space containing unconditional cooperation, even if it is a <Nash equilibrium>. This counterexample is valid for finite horizons and discounting as well as infinite averages.

= Long-run payoff of reactive strategies
{parent=Reactive strategy (game theory)}
{title2=$x_*=[q_1+(p_1-q_1)q_2]/[1-(p_1-q_1)(p_2-q_2)]$}

With initially <independent> actions, same-round <independence> is preserved by reactive updates: each next action uses the other <independent> previous action and fresh <independent> randomization. Marginal cooperation follows $x'=q_1+(p_1-q_1)y$, $y'=q_2+(p_2-q_2)x$. If the product of response slopes has <modulus> below one, these recurrences converge and the joint distribution is their product. Averaging $R,S,T,P$ over that distribution gives the long-time <payoffs>. At deterministic response slopes of <modulus> one, initial-memory-specific cycles must instead be averaged directly.

= Evolutionary game theory
{parent=Game theory}

<Evolutionary game theory> studies how a population's composition changes when the success of a <pure strategy> depends on which other <pure strategies> it encounters. The central feedback is a <frequency-dependent payoff>: becoming common changes the opponents a strategy meets, which can change whether it does well. In a <coordination game>, common behaviour can reinforce itself; in the coexistence example below, being rare is an advantage. This makes <evolutionary game theory> useful for asking which behaviours can persist and which initial compositions lead to different outcomes, without assuming that each participant solves an <optimization> problem.

In the basic model, a large, well-mixed population has finitely many inherited <pure strategies>; interactions sample opponents according to their population frequencies. Assume that a strategy's <expected payoff> determines its per capita net reproductive growth rate, with a common baseline allowed. These are modelling assumptions: reproduction must transmit the strategy, encounters must match the proposed sampling rule, and the conversion from <payoff> to growth must be justified for an application. The <replicator equation> below describes the resulting continuous-time frequencies. Taylor and Jonker's https://static1.squarespace.com/static/5dc9917c4cdb1467e1e54c4c/t/6526a5647942e33b89ab7af1/1697031525653/1978%2Bevolutionarily%2Bstable%2Bstrategies%2Band%2Bgame%2Bdynamics.pdf[Evolutionarily Stable Strategies and Game Dynamics (1978), section 3, pp. 148–149]{ref} explicitly derive this growth model.

For learned behaviour, reproduction can instead be replaced by an assumed copying rule: an individual samples another individual uniformly and copies a better-performing <pure strategy> at a rate proportional to the positive <payoff> difference. At frequencies $x_i,x_j$, the net flow from $j$ to $i$ is $x_ix_j(f_i-f_j)$, because the two directional positive parts subtract to $f_i-f_j$. Summing over $j$ gives $x_i(f_i-\bar f)$, the <replicator equation>. This derivation states a particular mechanism; an arbitrary rule for learning or imitation need not give the same <dynamical system>.

Three questions must be distinguished. A <symmetric Nash equilibrium> tests whether an individual can improve its <expected payoff> against the resident composition. An <evolutionarily stable strategy> tests whether each distinct rare mutant is outperformed after it is introduced. <Dynamical stability> tests the response of an actual <dynamical system> to small changes of composition, and <asymptotic stability> additionally requires nearby trajectories to converge. For a finite <symmetric bimatrix game> under the continuous-time <replicator equation>, every <evolutionarily stable strategy> is a <symmetric Nash equilibrium> and a <locally asymptotically stable> rest point; the converse from <asymptotic stability> to an <evolutionarily stable strategy> fails in general. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4113915/[Ross Cressman and Yi Tao, The replicator equation and other game dynamics (2014), Theorems 1–2 and Example 1]. These statements refer to stability on the full <probability simplex>, including perturbations introducing currently absent <pure strategies>.

A concrete experiment makes the frequency feedback visible. In 2009, Gore, Youk and van Oudenaarden competed two <yeast> strains: a producer of the <enzyme> invertase, which enables <sucrose> <hydrolysis>, and a mutant lacking the corresponding SUC2 <gene>. Released sugars can feed neighbours, so avoiding production can be advantageous. They used shaken 5 mL cultures with 5% <sucrose> at $30\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, diluted daily after 23 hours of growth. Different labelled <proteins> allowed the two strains to be counted separately by flow cytometry; selective plating periodically checked the counts. The cooperator required externally supplied histidine, so restricting that <amino acid> imposed an adjustable growth disadvantage. See https://www.youklab.org/papers/Nature2009_GoreAndYouk.pdf[Snowdrift game dynamics and facultative cheating in yeast, pp. 253 and 255–256, Methods Summary].

Starting with rare producers and, separately, rare nonproducers tested both invasion directions. Under the reported coexistence conditions each rare strain increased; changing histidine or added <glucose> shifted the eventual composition. Their explanation used nonlinear growth benefits, rather than a fixed pairwise <payoff matrix>. This motivates testing both rarity regimes before inferring an <evolutionarily stable strategy> or fitting a <replicator equation>; it does not identify the illustrative tables below with measured <payoffs>. See https://www.youklab.org/papers/Nature2009_GoreAndYouk.pdf[Gore, Youk and van Oudenaarden, Figures 1–3].

An early motivation was understanding why animal contests can involve restraint even when escalation might win an individual encounter. Maynard Smith and Price's https://public.ek-cer.hu/~szabo/EGT20/library/maynard_n73.pdf[The Logic of Animal Conflict (1973), Nature 246, 15–18]{ref} introduced the <evolutionarily stable strategy> in this setting. Taylor and Jonker's https://static1.squarespace.com/static/5dc9917c4cdb1467e1e54c4c/t/6526a5647942e33b89ab7af1/1697031525653/1978%2Bevolutionarily%2Bstable%2Bstrategies%2Band%2Bgame%2Bdynamics.pdf[1978 paper]{ref} then studied explicit continuous and discrete dynamics. Static resistance to invasion and the paths populations follow are related questions, but they require separate definitions.

= Signalling equilibrium
{parent=Evolutionary game theory}

= Conditional strategy
{parent=Evolutionary game theory}

A rule prescribing different actions in different states or histories. Conditionality does not require conscious calculation.

= Replicator equation
{parent=Evolutionary game theory}
{title2=$\dot x_i=x_i[(Ax)_i-x^TAx]$}

= Replicator dynamics
{synonym}

The <replicator equation> says that a strategy grows in frequency when its <expected payoff> exceeds the population's current <mean> <payoff>. It is an <ordinary differential equation> for a composition, rather than for total population size. The subtraction of the <mean> is what makes relative success matter: even a type growing in absolute number can shrink as a fraction if other types grow faster.

Let $x_i(t)$ be the frequency of <pure strategy> $i$, with $n$ available <pure strategies> and
$$
x\in\Delta_n=\left\{x\in\mathbb R^n:x_i\ge0,\ \sum_{i=1}^n x_i=1\right\}.
$$
In a <symmetric bimatrix game>, let the <payoff matrix> $A$ have entry $a_{ij}$ equal to the <payoff> to strategy $i$ against $j$. The other player's <payoff> in that encounter is $a_{ji}$: symmetry between player roles does not require $A=A^T$. Uniform random encounters give the <expected payoffs> and their population <mean>
$$
f_i(x)=(Ax)_i=\sum_j a_{ij}x_j,\qquad \bar f(x)=\sum_i x_if_i(x)=x^TAx.
$$
After choosing the time unit so that the growth-rate scale is one, the continuous-time <replicator equation> is
$$
\dot x_i=x_i\bigl(f_i(x)-\bar f(x)\bigr)=x_i\bigl((Ax)_i-x^TAx\bigr),\qquad i=1,\ldots,n.
$$
Here a <payoff> is measured on a scale proportional to net growth per unit time, not merely as an arbitrary preference ranking. More generally one can specify <locally Lipschitz> <frequency-dependent payoffs> $f_i(x)$ directly; the same equation with $\bar f=\sum_i x_if_i$ then defines a unique frequency trajectory. The linear pairwise model and its reproductive interpretation are given in https://static1.squarespace.com/static/5dc9917c4cdb1467e1e54c4c/t/6526a5647942e33b89ab7af1/1697031525653/1978%2Bevolutionarily%2Bstable%2Bstrategies%2Band%2Bgame%2Bdynamics.pdf[Taylor and Jonker (1978), sections 2–3]; the formulas here also follow by the following direct normalization.

Suppose the number $N_i$ of type $i$ obeys $\dot N_i=N_i[b(t)+f_i(x)]$, where the baseline $b(t)$ is identical for all types. Set $N=\sum_iN_i>0$ and $x_i=N_i/N$. The <quotient rule> gives
$$
\dot N=N[b(t)+\bar f(x)],\qquad \dot x_i=\frac{\dot N_iN-N_i\dot N}{N^2}=x_i(f_i-\bar f).
$$
Thus the common baseline cancels. Adding the same constant to every entry of $A$ leaves the <replicator equation> unchanged; multiplying $A$ by a positive constant changes only the speed along trajectories. The same <payoff matrix> can therefore describe frequency evolution at different growth-rate baselines and time scales.

The <probability simplex> is invariant in forward time. On $\sum_i x_i=1$,
$$
\sum_i\dot x_i=\sum_i x_if_i-\bar f\sum_i x_i=0.
$$
Normalization alone would not exclude negative frequencies. Nonnegativity follows as well: along a solution,
$$
x_i(t)=x_i(0)\exp\left(\int_0^t [f_i(x(s))-\bar f(x(s))]\,ds\right).
$$
An absent type remains absent, and a positive frequency remains positive at every finite time. For the fixed <payoff matrix> model, bounded rates on the compact <probability simplex> give global forward solutions. Frequencies can still tend to zero as $t\to\infty$. Each face is invariant, so this model has no spontaneous introduction of new <pure strategies>; <mutation> or outside introductions would require additional terms. For two present types the <logarithm> of their frequency ratio satisfies $\frac{d}{dt}\log(x_i/x_j)=f_i-f_j$, directly expressing the accumulated <payoff> advantage.

For two <pure strategies>, write the composition as $(x,1-x)$, where $x$ is the frequency of strategy 1, and let
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix},\qquad f_1=ax+b(1-x),\qquad f_2=cx+d(1-x).
$$
Their <payoff> difference and the resulting scalar <ordinary differential equation> are
$$
\Delta f(x)=f_1-f_2=(b-d)+(a-b-c+d)x,\qquad \dot x=F(x)=x(1-x)\Delta f(x).
$$
In the interior $0<x<1$, the sign of the <payoff> difference determines the direction of motion. The endpoints are always <equilibrium points>, and an additional interior <equilibrium point> exists precisely when the affine <payoff> difference has a zero strictly between them. At a simple interior zero $x_*$, $F'(x_*)=x_*(1-x_*)\Delta f'(x_*)$: a negative value gives <asymptotic stability>, a positive value gives <instability>. At the endpoints $F'(0)=b-d$ and $F'(1)=c-a$, with stability understood relative to $[0,1]$. A zero <derivative> requires examining the actual flow rather than this strict sign test.

For an original <coordination game>, imagine two conventions that reward matching, with convention 1 giving the larger matching reward. Choose
$$
A_{\mathrm{coord}}=\begin{pmatrix}3&0\\0&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad f_1=3x,\quad f_2=2(1-x),\quad \dot x=x(1-x)(5x-2).
$$
The crossing is $x_*=2/5$. Below it, convention 1 loses frequency; above it, convention 1 gains frequency. The endpoint <equilibrium points> $0$ and $1$ have one-sided <asymptotic stability>, while $2/5$ is unstable: the corresponding <derivatives> are $-2,-3$ and $6/5$, respectively. Every initial $0<x(0)<2/5$ tends to $0$, every initial $2/5<x(0)<1$ tends to $1$, and exactly $x(0)=2/5$ stays there. Even the convention with the larger matching reward can disappear if it starts below the threshold. This is <bistability>, not selection of a universally best convention.

\Image[replicator-coordination.png]
{title=Toy coordination model: an unstable frequency threshold separates attraction to the two pure conventions}

The compositions giving a <symmetric Nash equilibrium> in this <coordination game> are the two pure conventions and the mixed composition $(2/5,3/5)$. Both pure conventions are <evolutionarily stable strategies> because a mutant convention has strictly lower <payoff> against the pure resident. The mixed <symmetric Nash equilibrium> is not an <evolutionarily stable strategy>: against that resident both conventions tie, but after a pure convention-1 mutant is introduced its <payoff> advantage is $5(x-2/5)>0$. Equal <payoffs> at a <symmetric Nash equilibrium> need not provide resistance to invasion or <asymptotic stability>.

For an original coexistence model, choose <payoffs> making each convention advantageous when sufficiently rare:
$$
A_{\mathrm{coex}}=\begin{pmatrix}1&3\\2&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad f_1=3-2x,\quad f_2=2x,\quad \dot x=x(1-x)(3-4x).
$$
Here $x_*=3/4$ attracts every initial composition with both types present: $\dot x>0$ below it and $\dot x<0$ above it. The interior <derivative> is $F'(3/4)=-3/4$, while $F'(0)=3$ and $F'(1)=1$ make both endpoints unstable to introduction of the other type. The endpoints nevertheless remain stationary if the other type is exactly absent; the attraction statement excludes those boundary initial states.

\Image[replicator-coexistence.png]
{title=Toy coexistence model: both rare types can invade, and every interior composition approaches frequency three quarters}

The sole <symmetric Nash equilibrium> in this coexistence model is $p=(3/4,1/4)$. It is also an <evolutionarily stable strategy>. For any different <mixed strategy> $q=(y,1-y)$, the first comparison in the <two-condition criterion for evolutionary stability> ties because $Ap=(3/2,3/2)$. Its second comparison is strictly positive:
$$
p^TAq-q^TAq=(p-q)^TAq=(3/4-y)(3-4y)=4(y-3/4)^2>0.
$$
Thus restoring frequency feedback can protect a mixture even though all present types earn equal <payoffs> at the mixture. Coexistence here means different individuals using different <pure strategies>; it does not require every individual to randomize with a <mixed strategy>.

Frequency evolution need not increase the <mean> <payoff>. In the coexistence example $\bar f=5x-4x^2$, whose maximum occurs at $x=5/8$, whereas the attracting composition is $3/4$. For $5/8<x<3/4$, the frequency $x$ increases while $\frac{d}{dt}\bar f=(5-8x)\dot x<0$. Individual <payoff> advantages drive the equation; maximizing collective <mean> <payoff> is a different <optimization> problem.

More generally, a rest point only equalizes <payoffs> among present types: $x_i>0$ implies $(Ax)_i=x^TAx$. A <symmetric Nash equilibrium> additionally requires $(Ax)_i\le x^TAx$ for every absent type. All vertices are rest points even when an absent type could invade. For finite <symmetric bimatrix games>, a rest point with <Lyapunov stability> on the full <probability simplex> is a <symmetric Nash equilibrium>; an interior trajectory that converges has a <symmetric Nash equilibrium> as its limit. These are conditional results, not a convergence theorem for every trajectory. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4113915/[Cressman and Tao (2014), Theorem 1].

The <rock paper scissors> <payoff matrix> gives an explicit obstruction to universal convergence:
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&1\\1&0&-1\\-1&1&0\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Since $A^T=-A$, $x^TAx=0$, and in the interior $\frac{d}{dt}\log(x_1x_2x_3)=\sum_i(Ax)_i=0$. Thus $x_1x_2x_3$ is conserved. Its maximum on the <probability simplex> occurs only at $(1/3,1/3,1/3)$, the unique <symmetric Nash equilibrium>, so an interior trajectory starting elsewhere cannot converge to that composition. Every other interior product level is a closed curve without a rest point, giving a <periodic orbit>. The existence of these cycles is also discussed in https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4113915/[Cressman and Tao (2014), Example 1]. The useful lesson is to inspect the specified <payoff> feedback and <dynamical system>, rather than infer convergence from the existence of an <evolutionarily stable strategy> or a <Nash equilibrium>.

= Frequency-dependent payoff
{parent=Evolutionary game theory}
{title2=$f_i(x)$}

A <frequency-dependent payoff> is an <expected payoff> depending on the population composition $x$. Under random pairwise encounters with a fixed <payoff matrix> it is the <linear function> $f_i(x)=(Ax)_i$. The relative advantage $f_i(x)-f_j(x)$ can change sign as opponents become more or less common. The <replicator equation> converts this feedback into changes of frequency; nonlinear <frequency-dependent payoffs> allow interactions not represented by a fixed pairwise table.

= Repeated game
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

A <repeated game> plays a stage game over several rounds, allowing actions to depend on earlier observations. Its <payoff> convention must specify a finite horizon, a discount factor or a long-time average; these need not have identical invasion properties.

= Finite-state strategy
{parent=Repeated game}

A history-dependent <pure strategy> represented by a <finite-state machine> with an initial <automaton state>, an action output for each state, and an <automaton transition> after each observed completed joint action. A <grim trigger strategy> uses a following state and an absorbing punishment state. Two such deterministic machines jointly form a finite deterministic state system, so their action path is eventually periodic. Its <long-run average payoffs> are the averages over the eventual cycle; a finite initial prefix has vanishing weight.

= Stage game
{parent=Repeated game}

The <normal-form game> played once in each round of a <repeated game>. Its actions and <payoffs> are distinct from the repeated interaction's history-dependent <pure strategies> and aggregate <payoffs>. The same <stage game> can produce different incentive constraints under a finite sum, discounting or <long-run average payoff> criterion.

= Folk theorem for repeated games
{parent=Repeated game}

A family of results describing the large set of <Nash equilibrium> <payoffs> available in indefinitely repeated interaction. For a finite game with observed past play, the discounted Nash version supports any feasible, strictly <individually rational payoff in a repeated game> when players are sufficiently patient.<Lecture 15: Repeated Games by Asu Ozdaglar>[Slide 24, Nash folk theorem; slides 25–27, permanent minmax punishments and the distinction from subgame perfection]{ref}

The mechanism is to prescribe a path and punish the first deviator by holding that player's future <payoff> to its <minmax payoff>. With <long-run average payoffs>, a finite gain disappears from the average; with discounting, the promised future loss must outweigh the gain. Weak boundary points require care when a stated theorem assumes strict inequalities. A construction proving a <Nash equilibrium> need not prove a <subgame perfect equilibrium>, because the punishers' continuation incentives may fail.

= Individually rational payoff in a repeated game
{parent=Repeated game}

= Individually rational payoffs in a repeated game
{synonym}

A <payoff> vector giving every player at least that player's <minmax payoff>. It is strictly individually rational if every inequality is strict. A player can reject an equilibrium offering less than the guaranteed amount by choosing a securing strategy instead. These bounds concern repeated-game incentives; they are distinct from participation constraints in <mechanism design>.

= Minmax payoff
{parent=Repeated game}
{title2=$\underline v_i=\min_{\sigma_{-i}}\max_{\sigma_i}U_i(\sigma_i,\sigma_{-i})$}

The lowest <expected payoff> to which a player's opponents can hold that player when the player chooses a <best response>. The opponents' permitted randomization and correlation conventions should be specified for games with several opponents. In a two-player finite game, this equals the player's maximum guaranteed <expected payoff> by the <minimax theorem> applied to that player's payoff matrix. This equality does not make the original two-player game a <zero-sum game>.

= Feasible payoff set
{parent=Repeated game}
{title2=$\operatorname{conv}\{u(a):a\text{ a stage action profile}\}$}

The <convex hull> of the stage game's attainable <payoff> vectors. Public randomization or deterministic sequences with prescribed limiting frequencies produce such average <payoffs>. It can be larger than the set attainable using independent <mixed strategies> in a single stage.

= Long-run average payoff
{parent=Repeated game}
{title2=$\lim_{T\to\infty}T^{-1}\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}u_i^t$}

A <repeated game> criterion evaluating the average stage <payoff> over increasingly long horizons. State how paths without a limit are treated, for example by using a lower limit. Changing a finite prefix does not change a convergent <long-run average payoff>. Thus a one-time gain can be outweighed by permanent future punishment without specifying a discount factor.

= Payoff
{parent=Game theory}
{title2=$u_i(s_1,\ldots,s_n)$}

A <payoff> is the number assigned to a player's outcome at a <strategy profile>, with larger numbers preferred. In the usual <expected utility> model, <expected payoffs> are <probability>-weighted averages of these numbers. If a <payoff> is specified as money and a player has preferences involving risk, first apply the relevant <utility function>; maximizing expected money assumes <risk neutrality>. In a two-player <zero-sum game>, one player's <payoff> is the negative of the other's. General <bimatrix games> instead require a <payoff matrix> for each player.

= Expected payoff
{parent=Payoff}
{title2=$U_i(\sigma)=\sum_s u_i(s)\prod_j\sigma_j(s_j)$}

For a <finite game>, let $s=(s_1,\ldots,s_n)$ range over all <pure-strategy> <strategy profiles>, and let $\sigma_j(s_j)$ be player $j$'s <probability> of choosing $s_j$. Under <independent> <mixed strategies>, the <probability> of $s$ is $\prod_j\sigma_j(s_j)$. The displayed sum is the <expected value> of player $i$'s <payoff>. Holding everyone else's <mixed strategy> fixed makes $U_i$ a <linear function> of $\sigma_i$; this is why checking <pure-strategy> deviations suffices to check all <mixed-strategy> deviations.

= Costly turnout game
{parent=Game theory}

Each voter chooses whether to incur a voting cost. The collective result changes each voter's payoff, and abstention can be optimal when other voters are likely to determine the outcome. The payoff difference between voting and abstaining is the benefit of being pivotal, weighted by the pivotal probability, minus the turnout cost.

// Target: mathematical-optimization.bigb

= Turnout equilibria with two supporters and one opponent
{parent=Costly turnout game}

Suppose turnout costs $3c>0$, each supporter gains $4c$ on passage, and the opponent loses $8c$. The symmetric-supporter <Nash equilibria> $(\alpha,\beta,\gamma)$ are $(0,1/4,1/4)$ and $(1,3/4,3/4)$. Allowing different supporter probabilities adds $(1/4,0,3/8)$ and $(1/4,3/8,0)$. These follow by comparing the <pivotal voting probabilities> with the turnout cost; no player benefits by changing their mixed strategy.

// Target: mathematical-optimization.bigb

= Pivotal voting probability
{parent=Costly turnout game}

A voter is pivotal when changing only that voter's participation changes the election result. For an opponent facing two independent supporters with participation probabilities $\beta,\gamma$, under a rule requiring strictly more votes in favour, the opponent is pivotal when exactly one supporter votes. This probability is $\beta(1-\gamma)+(1-\beta)\gamma$.

// Target: mathematical-optimization.bigb

= Nonatomic congestion game
{parent=Game theory}

= Nonatomic routing game
{synonym}

In a nonatomic congestion game, a divisible population chooses resources whose costs depend on aggregate usage. Each individual has negligible size, so changing their choice does not alter those costs. For traffic on a <flow network>, strategies are routes and an equilibrium is a <Wardrop equilibrium>: every route with positive flow has minimum delay for its source-sink pair. This differs from a finite-player <congestion game>, where a deviation changes the load used to evaluate the deviator's new cost. Continuous nondecreasing link delays give a <Beckmann potential> whose minimizers are exactly the traffic equilibria.

= Congestion game
{parent=Game theory}

Players select sets of resources, and each resource's cost depends on its total number of users. Each player's cost is the sum of the costs of their selected resources. A unilateral deviation changes resource loads, so a finite-player <Nash equilibrium> compares costs after that change, not at the old loads. For cost functions $c_e$, the potential $\sum_e\sum_{j=1}^{n_e}c_e(j)$ changes by exactly the deviator's cost change. A minimizer of this potential in a <finite game> is therefore a pure <Nash equilibrium>.

= Evolutionarily stable strategy
{parent=Game theory}
{title2=$e(x^*,(1-\epsilon)x^*+\epsilon y)>e(y,(1-\epsilon)x^*+\epsilon y)$}

= ESS
{c}
{synonym}

A resident strategy is evolutionarily stable if every distinct mutant earns lower expected fitness when introduced at sufficiently small positive frequency. The upper bound on the frequency may depend on the mutant. In a finite symmetric matrix game, $e(x,y)=x^TAy$ is the expected payoff, with the second argument representing the population strategy. Affineness in this argument yields the <two-condition criterion for evolutionary stability>. Every evolutionarily stable strategy is a symmetric <Nash equilibrium>, but a tied best response must also be strictly outperformed against its own mutant population. The <Hawk-Dove game> supplies an interior stable mixture despite equality of all payoffs against the resident mixture.

= Selection gradient
{parent=Evolutionarily stable strategy}
{title2=$g(x)=\partial_y f(y,x)|_{y=x}$}

For a differentiable rare-mutant invasion fitness $f(y,x)$ against resident trait $x$, the <selection gradient> gives the first-order growth advantage of a nearby mutant. A zero gradient is a candidate interior evolutionary singularity. Strict local uninvadability follows from negative mutant-fitness curvature; evolutionary approach additionally requires the gradient to point toward the candidate. Boundary traits require one-sided tests. A gradient alone does not prove global evolutionary stability.

= Hawk-Dove game
{c}
{parent=Evolutionarily stable strategy}
{title2=$e(p,q)=V(1-q)/2+p(V-Dq)/2$}

Hawk fights for a resource of value $V$, while Dove retreats from Hawk and shares with another Dove. Hawk–Hawk competition incurs damage cost $D$. The expected payoff difference between Hawk probabilities $p,r$ against population probability $q$ is $(p-r)(V-Dq)/2$. For positive $V,D$, pure Hawk is an <evolutionarily stable strategy> when $V>D$. At $V=D$, it remains stable because a mutant $p<1$ loses $V(1-p)^2/2$ against its own population. When $0<V<D$, the stable mixture has Hawk probability $p^*=V/D$: all first-condition payoffs tie, and its second-condition advantage is $D(p-p^*)^2/2>0$. For nonpositive resource values, pure Dove is stable if $V<0$ or if $V=0<D$. At $V=D=0$ all payoffs vanish and there is no evolutionarily stable strategy.

= Chicken payoff ordering
{parent=Hawk-Dove game}
{title2=$T>R>S>P$}

The <Chicken payoff ordering> describes a symmetric two-player <bimatrix game> in which aggression earns temptation $T$ against yielding, mutual yielding earns $R$, yielding to aggression earns $S$, and mutual aggression earns $P$, with the displayed strict ordering. Aggression is a <best response> to yielding, and yielding is a <best response> to aggression. Thus the two asymmetric action pairs are <pure strategy> <Nash equilibria>. The <Hawk-Dove game> has this ordering in its positive-resource regime where the damage cost exceeds the resource value. In the <prisoner's dilemma>, the ordering of $S$ and $P$ is reversed, making defection a <strictly dominant strategy> instead.

= Two-condition criterion for evolutionary stability
{parent=Evolutionarily stable strategy}
{title2=$A_y\geq0,\quad A_y=0\Longrightarrow B_y>0$}

For mixed-strategy expected payoffs put $A_y=e(x^*,x^*)-e(y,x^*)$ and $B_y=e(x^*,y)-e(y,y)$. The invasion payoff difference is exactly $(1-\epsilon)A_y+\epsilon B_y$. Positivity for all sufficiently small positive $\epsilon$ forces $A_y\geq0$ by taking the limit. When $A_y=0$, it forces $B_y>0$. Conversely $A_y>0$ makes the difference positive for sufficiently small frequency, and $A_y=0,B_y>0$ makes it positive for every positive frequency. Apply this separately to every distinct mutant. This proves the equivalence with an <evolutionarily stable strategy>; affineness in population composition is essential to the formula.

= Security level payoff
{parent=Game theory}
{title2=$d_1=\max_s\min_j(s^TP)_j$}

A player maximizes the worst payoff it can receive against the other player's actions. Allowing the opponent to mix does not lower the worst payoff beyond a pure action, since the payoff is linear in that mixture. A <security level payoff> is an individual guarantee, not generally a <Nash equilibrium> payoff.

= Nash bargaining problem
{c}
{parent=Game theory}
{title2=$(F,d)$}

The essential two-person bargaining domain consists of a <compact convex set> of feasible utility vectors $F$, a <disagreement point> $d\in F$, and at least one feasible vector strictly exceeding $d$ coordinatewise. The <Nash bargaining solution> selects a jointly feasible improvement using a rule characterized by <Pareto efficiency>, <bargaining symmetry>, <positive affine invariance in bargaining> and <bargaining independence of irrelevant alternatives>.

= Negotiation set in two-person bargaining
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}
{title2=$B=\{u\in F:u\ge d,\ u\text{ is Pareto optimal}\}$}

= Negotiation set
{synonym}

For a <compact convex set> of feasible <payoff> <vectors> $F$ and a specified <disagreement point> $d$, the <negotiation set> is its individually rational <Pareto frontier>. It is the set of efficient agreements each player prefers at least weakly to disagreement. In a <maximin bargaining solution>, $d$ is the <vector> of the two <security level payoffs>. This usage is distinct from objection-and-counterobjection bargaining sets in coalitional-game theory.

= Joint dominance of payoff vectors
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}
{title2=$u'\ge u,\quad u'\ne u$}

A feasible <payoff> <vector> $u'$ jointly dominates $u$ if every coordinate is at least as large and at least one is strictly larger. A <Pareto optimal> <vector> has no feasible joint dominator. Strict increase of every coordinate is not required. This compares each player's own utilities, not the numerical size of utilities across different players.

= Bargaining individual rationality
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}
{title2=$u\geq d$}

A payoff is individually rational for a <Nash bargaining problem> when neither player receives less than its <disagreement point> payoff. Essentiality permits strict improvement for both. Maximizing a positive <Nash product> therefore selects a strictly individually rational payoff.

= Bargaining independence of irrelevant alternatives
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}

If an admissible smaller feasible set with the same <disagreement point> contains the original chosen vector, deleting the other outcomes must leave the choice unchanged. The <Nash bargaining solution> satisfies this axiom because its unique product maximizer remains feasible.

= Positive affine invariance in bargaining
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}
{title2=$u_i\mapsto a_i u_i+b_i,\quad a_i>0$}

Transforming each utility by an independent positive affine map must transform the chosen payoff by the same map. The <Nash product> changes only by a positive factor, so the <Nash bargaining solution> has this invariance.

= Bargaining symmetry
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}

When the feasible payoff set and <disagreement point> are invariant under interchange of players, the solution must give both players the same payoff.

= Nash bargaining solution
{c}
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}
{title2=$\operatorname*{argmax}_{u\in F,\ u\geq d}(u_1-d_1)(u_2-d_2)$}

= Nash arbitration procedure
{c}
{synonym}

For an essential <Nash bargaining problem>, maximizing the <Nash product> gives a unique vector: on positive gains its logarithm is a <strictly concave function>. The rule satisfies the four bargaining axioms, and the <supporting triangle for Nash bargaining> proves their converse characterization. The nonessential case requires additional conventions, since a zero product may have many maximizers.

= Maximin bargaining solution
{parent=Nash bargaining solution}
{title2=$\operatorname*{argmax}_{u\in F,\ u\ge d}(u_1-d_1)(u_2-d_2)$}

For a finite two-person game, let $d_i$ be player $i$'s <security level payoff>, obtained by maximizing its minimum expected <payoff> against the other player's actions. Apply the <Nash bargaining solution> to the convexified feasible <payoff> region with this <disagreement point>. This is the <maximin bargaining solution>. “Maximin” specifies the disagreement guarantees; the arbitration <objective function> remains the <Nash product>, not the minimum of two numerically incomparable utilities.

= Supporting triangle for Nash bargaining
{parent=Nash bargaining solution}
{title2=$z_1,z_2\geq-M,\quad z_1+z_2\leq2$}

Normalize a positive <Nash product> maximizer and the <disagreement point> to $(1,1)$ and zero. The first-order product inequality along every feasible segment places the transformed set in $z_1+z_2\leq2$. Compactness allows a symmetric containing triangle with finite lower coordinate bounds. <Bargaining symmetry> and <Pareto efficiency> choose $(1,1)$ in that triangle; <bargaining independence of irrelevant alternatives> transfers this choice to the original normalized set. <Positive affine invariance in bargaining> then proves the full characterization.

= Nash product
{c}
{parent=Nash bargaining solution}
{title2=$(u_1-d_1)(u_2-d_2)$}

The product of the two players' gains over the <disagreement point> is maximized over the individually rational feasible set in the <Nash bargaining solution>. Positive affine changes of utility multiply this product by a positive constant and leave the selected payoff correspondence unchanged after transforming coordinates.

= Disagreement point
{parent=Nash bargaining problem}
{title2=$d$}

= Threat point
{synonym}

The disagreement point specifies each player's payoff if bargaining fails. It supplies the baseline from which the <Nash product> measures gains. In a game-based example it may be set to the vector of <security level payoffs>; this choice must be recomputed when the underlying payoff matrices change.

= Cooperative game theory
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

Cooperative game theory studies what groups of players can achieve together and how their joint value can be allocated. A <transferable utility game> assigns a real value to each <coalition>. The <core of a cooperative game> asks which allocations no <coalition> can improve upon; the <Shapley value> averages <marginal contributions> across player orderings.

= Transferable utility game
{parent=Cooperative game theory}
{title2=$v:2^N\to\mathbb R,\quad v(\varnothing)=0$}

= Coalitional game
{synonym}

A finite transferable utility game gives each <coalition> $S\subseteq N$ a real value $v(S)$ that its members can distribute among themselves. The usual normalization is $v(\varnothing)=0$. An efficient payoff vector $x$ satisfies $\sum_{i\in N}x_i=v(N)$. <Simple cooperative games> model winning <coalitions> with values zero and one; <convex cooperative games> model increasing <marginal contributions>.

= Cooperative cost game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$p(N)=c(N),\quad p(S)\leq c(S)$}

A <transferable utility game> formulated as costs $c(S)$ to be shared among members of each <coalition> $S$, with $c(\varnothing)=0$. Its cost <core> consists of payment <vectors> $p$ satisfying the displayed efficiency and coalition inequalities, where $p(S)=\sum_{i\in S}p_i$. A coalition should not be charged more than its standalone cost. Changing signs to $v=-c$ and $x=-p$ converts these conditions to the usual benefit-game <core of a cooperative game>. The <Shapley value> can be applied directly to costs, giving payments whose sum is $c(N)$.

= Airport problem
{parent=Cooperative cost game}
{wiki}

= Airport game
{synonym}

Share a runway's cost among users needing different runway lengths. If user $i$ needs a facility costing $C_i\geq0$, a nonempty <coalition> pays $c(S)=\max_{i\in S}C_i$, and $c(\varnothing)=0$. This is a <subadditive cost game> because the maximum of two nonnegative costs does not exceed their sum. A cost-core allocation always exists: charge the whole cost to one longest-runway user and zero to all others. The <Shapley value of an airport game> gives a symmetric alternative that also lies in the cost <core>.

= Shapley value of an airport game
{c}
{parent=Airport problem}
{title2=$\phi_j=\lambda\sum_{i=1}^j\frac{s_i-s_{i-1}}{N_i}$}

Suppose the distinct required runway lengths are $0=s_0<s_1<\cdots<s_k$, with $n_i>0$ users of type $i$, constant cost $\lambda\geq0$ per unit length, and suffix counts $N_i=\sum_{\ell=i}^k n_\ell$. Then every type-$j$ user has the displayed <Shapley value>. For layer $i$, exactly $N_i$ users require it. The first of them in a uniformly random <permutation> contributes its entire cost $\lambda(s_i-s_{i-1})$. Each requiring user has <probability> $1/N_i$ of being first. <Linearity of the Shapley value> sums these expected <marginal contributions> across the layers that user needs.

The allocation lies in the cost <core>. Its payments sum to the full runway cost. For any <coalition> $S$, its total payment toward layer $i$ is $\lambda(s_i-s_{i-1})|S\cap R_i|/N_i$, where $R_i$ is the set of users requiring that layer. This is zero if $S$ needs no such layer, and at most the full layer cost otherwise. Summing gives $\sum_{j\in S}\phi_j\leq c(S)$. Suffix counts followed by prefix sums compute all distinct type payments in $O(k)$ arithmetic operations.

= Subadditive cost game
{parent=Cooperative cost game}
{title2=$c(S\cup T)\leq c(S)+c(T)\quad(S\cap T=\varnothing)$}

A <cooperative cost game> in which disjoint <coalitions> can merge without increasing their combined cost. Negating its cost function gives a <superadditive coalitional game>. Subadditivity by itself does not ensure a nonempty cost <core>.

= Additivity of a cooperative-game value
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$\psi(v+w)=\psi(v)+\psi(w)$}

= Cooperative-game additivity
{synonym}

A value has <cooperative-game additivity> when adding two characteristic <functions> on the same player <set> adds their allocation <vectors>. Here $(v+w)(S)=v(S)+w(S)$ for every <coalition>. The sum of two <simple cooperative games> need not be simple; the axiom is naturally stated on <transferable utility games>.

= Null player
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S\cup\{i\})=v(S)\quad\text{for every }S\subseteq N\setminus\{i\}$}

A <null player> has zero <marginal contribution> to every <coalition>. The axiom for a <null player> requires allocating that player zero. This is the zero-marginal convention used here.

= Efficiency of a cooperative-game value
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$\sum_{i\in N}\psi_i(v)=v(N)$}

= Cooperative-game efficiency
{synonym}

A value assigning a <vector> of <payoffs> $\psi(v)$ to a <transferable utility game> has <cooperative-game efficiency> when it distributes exactly the grand-<coalition> value. This is a budget-total condition; it does not assert membership in the <core of a cooperative game>.

= Probabilistic Banzhaf index
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$\beta_i(v)=2^{-(n-1)}\sum_{S\subseteq N\setminus\{i\}}[v(S\cup\{i\})-v(S)]$}

= Banzhaf index
{c}
{synonym}

The <probabilistic Banzhaf index> averages a player's <marginal contribution> over all <coalitions> not containing that player, each given equal weight. In a <simple cooperative game>, it is the <probability> of changing a losing <coalition> into a winning one when every other player joins independently with <probability> $1/2$. Its <sum> over players need not equal the grand-<coalition> value: in a three-player majority game each player has index $1/2$, giving total $3/2$ despite $v(N)=1$. This convention differs from both the raw count of pivotal <coalitions> and the <normalized Banzhaf index>.

The name refers to <John Banzhaf>, whose 1965 paper addresses the mathematical analysis of weighted voting.<Weighted Voting Doesn't Work: A Mathematical Analysis by John Banzhaf>[Author's reference page, citation to Rutgers Law Review 19 (1965), starting p. 317]{ref}

= Banzhaf difference identity
{c}
{parent=Probabilistic Banzhaf index}
{title2=$\beta_j-\beta_i=2^{-(n-2)}\sum_{T\subseteq N\setminus\{i,j\}}[v(T\cup\{j\})-v(T\cup\{i\})]$}

For distinct players $i,j$, expand each <probabilistic Banzhaf index> by whether the sampled <coalition> contains the other player. The terms $v(T)$ and $v(T\cup\{i,j\})$ cancel on subtraction, leaving the displayed identity. In a <weighted voting game> with $w_i\leq w_j$, every summand is nonnegative, proving that the index is monotone in the player's weight. Equal weights give equal indices, although different weights can also have equal indices.

= Normalized Banzhaf index
{parent=Probabilistic Banzhaf index}
{title2=$\beta'_i(v)=v(N)\beta_i(v)/\sum_j\beta_j(v)$}

= Normalised Banzhaf index
{synonym}

The <normalized Banzhaf index> rescales the <probabilistic Banzhaf indices> to sum to the grand-<coalition> value, whenever the denominator is nonzero. It preserves <cooperative-game symmetry> and assigns zero to a <null player>, but generally fails <cooperative-game additivity>. For example, add the three-player majority game to a dictatorship of player one. Their normalized indices are $(1/3,1/3,1/3)$ and $(1,0,0)$, whereas the sum game has normalized index $(6/5,2/5,2/5)$, not their vector <sum>. The sum game has grand-<coalition> value two, which must be retained in the normalization.

= Symmetry of players in a cooperative game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S\cup\{i\})=v(S\cup\{j\})\quad(S\subseteq N\setminus\{i,j\})$}

Players $i,j$ are symmetric when interchanging them does not affect any <coalition> value. The displayed condition is equivalent to invariance under their transposition. If every pair is symmetric, arbitrary permutations preserve the game, and <coalitions> of equal <cardinality> have equal values. This is a property of the <transferable utility game>, rather than a requirement that a chosen <payoff> allocation treat players equally.

= Symmetry of a cooperative-game value
{parent=Symmetry of players in a cooperative game}

= Cooperative-game symmetry
{synonym}

A value has <cooperative-game symmetry> when it assigns equal <payoffs> to players satisfying <symmetry of players in a cooperative game>. Thus equality of their <marginal contributions> is reflected in the allocation, rather than merely being a property of the underlying game.

= Induced subgraph game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S)=\sum_{\{i,j\}\subseteq S}w_{ij}$}

A <transferable utility game> represented by a weighted <undirected graph>, with one player per <graph vertex>. A <coalition> receives the sum of weights of the <graph edges> in its <induced subgraph>; absent edges have weight zero. Singleton and empty <coalitions> have value zero. This is a representation of a <transferable utility game>, distinct from a graphical description of local <payoffs> in a <normal-form game>.

= Convexity criterion for an induced subgraph game
{parent=Induced subgraph game}
{title2=$v\text{ is supermodular}\iff w_{ij}\ge0\text{ for all }i\ne j$}

A loop-free <induced subgraph game> is a <convex cooperative game> exactly when all <edge weights> are nonnegative. Necessity follows by testing the <supermodular> inequality on two distinct singleton <coalitions>. For sufficiency,
$$
v(S\cup T)+v(S\cap T)-v(S)-v(T)
=\sum_{i\in S\setminus T,\ j\in T\setminus S}w_{ij}\ge0.
$$
Only <edges> connecting the two set differences survive cancellation. Loop contributions cancel as well, so the same off-diagonal condition remains sufficient and necessary when <self-loops> are added.

= Induced subgraph game with self-loops
{parent=Induced subgraph game}
{title2=$v(S)=\sum_{i\in S}\ell_i+\sum_{\{i,j\}\subseteq S,\ i\ne j}w_{ij}$}

An <induced subgraph game> extended by one <self-loop> weight $\ell_i$ per player. Each loop is counted once, so $v(\{i\})=\ell_i$ and
$$
w_{ij}=v(\{i,j\})-v(\{i\})-v(\{j\}).
$$
Singleton and pair <coalition> values determine all weights; larger <coalition> values must then agree with their sums. Signed weights are allowed. For example, two loops of weight $1$ joined by an <edge> of weight $-1$ represent the <simple cooperative game> in which any <coalition> meeting those two players wins.

= Shapley value of an induced subgraph game
{c}
{parent=Induced subgraph game}
{title2=$\phi_i(v)=\frac12\sum_{j\ne i}w_{ij}$}

In a uniformly random player <permutation>, edge $\{i,j\}$ contributes $w_{ij}$ to $i$'s <marginal contribution> exactly when $j$ precedes $i$, which occurs with <probability> $1/2$. Sum these expected contributions over incident <graph edges> to obtain the displayed <Shapley value>. This proof works for arbitrary real edge weights; nonnegativity is not required.

= Constant-sum cooperative game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S)+v(N\setminus S)=v(N)$}

A <transferable utility game> satisfying the displayed equality for every <coalition>. This forces $v(\varnothing)=0$. It does not by itself guarantee a nonempty <core>: <core rigidity in a constant-sum cooperative game> shows that nonemptiness requires the <characteristic function of a coalitional game> to be additive.

= Core rigidity in a constant-sum cooperative game
{parent=Constant-sum cooperative game}
{title2=$\operatorname{Core}(v)\ne\varnothing\ \Longrightarrow\ v(S)=\sum_{i\in S}v(\{i\})$}

For a <core> allocation $x$, the inequalities for $S$ and its complement, together with efficiency, give $x(S)\ge v(S)$ and $x(S)=v(N)-x(N\setminus S)\le v(N)-v(N\setminus S)=v(S)$. Thus equality holds for every <coalition>; singletons force $x_i=v(\{i\})$ and all other values must be their sums. Conversely, if the <characteristic function of a coalitional game> is additive, that unique allocation satisfies every <core> inequality with equality. Hence a <constant-sum cooperative game> has either an empty <core> or this one-point <core>.

= Empty-coalition normalization
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(\varnothing)=0$}

The usual convention that the empty <coalition> creates no value. It makes the sum of a <marginal contribution vector> equal $v(N)$ by a <telescoping sum>. Without it, that sum is $v(N)-v(\varnothing)$, so the vector need not satisfy efficiency in the <core of a cooperative game>. The constant function $v(T)=1$ is <supermodular> but gives a zero marginal vector and fails efficiency for a nonempty player set.

= Coverage coalitional game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S)=\left|\bigcup_{i\in S}B_i\right|$}

Players hold finite sets of resources, and their coalition value is the number of distinct resources they collectively cover. The value is generally subadditive rather than superadditive: overlapping resources are counted only once. It is a <superadditive coalitional game> exactly when the players' resource sets are pairwise disjoint.

= Shapley value of a coverage game
{c}
{parent=Coverage coalitional game}
{title2=$\phi_i(v)=\sum_{b\in B_i}|\{j:b\in B_j\}|^{-1}$}

Decompose a <coverage coalitional game> into one-resource games and use additivity of the <Shapley value>. A player contributes a resource precisely when it is the earliest player in a random ordering among those holding that resource. Each holder has probability the reciprocal of the number of holders of being earliest. Summing these contributions proves the formula, regardless of overlaps or emptiness of the core.

= Core of a coverage game
{parent=Coverage coalitional game}

A <coverage coalitional game> has a nonempty <core of a cooperative game> exactly when all resource sets are pairwise disjoint. Singleton coalition constraints require $x_i\geq|B_i|$, while efficiency requires $\sum_i x_i=|\bigcup_iB_i|$. Any overlap makes these inequalities inconsistent. When there is no overlap, the allocation $x_i=|B_i|$ is the unique core allocation and gives every coalition its value exactly.

= Bankruptcy game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S)=\max\{0,a-\sum_{i\notin S}c_i\}$}

With nonnegative claims $c_i$ and an estate $0\leq a\leq\sum_i c_i$, a coalition's guaranteed worth is the estate left after all outside claims are met, or zero if those claims exhaust it. Writing the total deficit $d=\sum_i c_i-a$ gives $v(S)=(\sum_{i\in S}c_i-d)_+$. For disjoint coalitions, $(p+q-d)_+\geq(p-d)_++(q-d)_+$, so this is a <superadditive coalitional game>. Its <core of a cooperative game> consists exactly of efficient allocations with $0\leq x_i\leq c_i$: the upper bounds are equivalent to the complementary-coalition constraints, and the lower bounds together with efficiency imply every other coalition constraint.

= Superadditive coalitional game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S\cup T)\geq v(S)+v(T)\quad(S\cap T=\varnothing)$}

The <characteristic function of a coalitional game> is superadditive when disjoint coalitions can attain at least the sum of their separate values by joining. This follows when their separate strategies can be played together without interference. Superadditivity is an additional property of an abstract <transferable utility game>, not a consequence of merely assigning values to subsets: $v(\{1\})=v(\{2\})=v(\{1,2\})=1$ is a normalized counterexample.

= Superadditivity and nonempty core do not imply Shapley stability
{parent=Superadditive coalitional game}

On three players, let $v(S)=1$ exactly when $S$ contains player 1 and at least one of players 2 and 3, and zero otherwise. This <simple cooperative game> is superadditive: disjoint <coalitions> cannot both contain player 1, so at most one can have value one. Its <core of a cooperative game> is exactly $\{(1,0,0)\}$, since singleton nonnegativity and the two winning-pair constraints force the other two coordinates to zero. In a random player ordering, player 1 is pivotal unless it comes first, whereas each other player is pivotal in just one of the six orders. Thus its <Shapley value> is $(2/3,1/6,1/6)$, outside the <core>: coalition $\{1,2\}$ receives only $5/6<1$. <Convex cooperative games> have the stronger property that their <Shapley value belongs to the core of a convex game>.

= Glove game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S)=\omega\min(|S\cap L|,|S\cap R|)$}

Players each own one object of one of two complementary types. A matched pair sells for $\omega>0$, and objects of one type alone have no value. The <characteristic function of a coalitional game> is therefore the displayed matching count times pair price. The <core of a glove game> pays the scarce type the entire pair value and the abundant type zero; if type counts are equal, a common split between the two types can vary continuously.

= Core of a glove game
{parent=Glove game}
{title2=$\ell_i=\theta,\quad r_j=\omega-\theta$}

If there are fewer left owners than right owners, deleting any right owner leaves the grand-coalition value unchanged. The complement's core constraint and efficiency force that owner's payoff to be zero. Mixed-pair inequalities then require every left owner to receive at least $\omega$, and efficiency makes all these payoffs exactly $\omega$. Reverse the types when right owners are scarce. If both counts equal $k$, sum all $k^2$ mixed-pair inequalities. Their total is exactly $k$ times the grand-coalition payoff, so each pair sum is $\omega$. Hence all left owners receive a common $\theta\in[0,\omega]$ and all right owners $\omega-\theta$. Such allocations meet every coalition constraint because $\theta u+(\omega-\theta)v\geq\omega\min(u,v)$. This proves the complete <core> description.

= Characteristic function of a coalitional game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v:2^N\to\mathbb R$}

The characteristic function assigns every <coalition> its total attainable transferable payoff, with $v(\varnothing)=0$. It determines the efficiency condition of an <imputation> and all constraints of the <core of a cooperative game>. For the <glove game>, $v(S)=\omega\min(|S\cap L|,|S\cap R|)$; for miners requiring $r$ people to carry one lump, $v(S)=\lfloor|S|/r\rfloor$. This game-theoretic function is distinct from the <characteristic function> of a probability distribution.

= Dual coalitional game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v'(S)=v(N)-v(N\setminus S)$}

Duality assigns a <coalition> the grand-coalition value less the value of its complement. For a normalized <transferable utility game>, the dual is normalized and taking the dual twice recovers the original game. A player's dual <marginal contribution> at predecessor set $S$ equals its original contribution at the complementary predecessor set in $N\setminus\{i\}$. This preserves the <Shapley value>.

= Nucleolus
{parent=Transferable utility game}

When the <imputation> set is nonempty, the <nucleolus> is the unique <imputation> lexicographically minimizing the vector of <excesses of a coalition> sorted decreasingly. It minimizes the largest complaint first, then subsequent complaints among ties. It can be computed through successive <linear programs> that minimize the current maximum excess and retain constraints fixed by prior stages. Its uniqueness is the standard <nucleolus> theorem. If the <core of a cooperative game> is nonempty, the <nucleolus> belongs to it; an empty core does not preclude a <nucleolus>.

= Prenucleolus
{parent=Nucleolus}

The <prenucleolus> uses the same sorted <excesses of a coalition> but minimizes over all efficient payoff vectors, without imposing individual rationality. It can differ from the <nucleolus> when <coalition> values are not superadditive. Any computation must state which domain is used rather than silently relaxing <imputation> inequalities.

= Imputation in a coalitional game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$I(v)=\{x:x(N)=v(N),\ x_i\geq v(\{i\})\}$}

= Imputation
{synonym}

An <imputation> is an efficient, individually rational allocation in a <transferable utility game>. Its total is the grand-coalition value and each player receives at least its singleton value. The set is nonempty exactly when $v(N)\geq\sum_i v(\{i\})$, and is then compact. This individual-rationality condition distinguishes the <nucleolus> from the <prenucleolus>.

= Marginal contribution
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$\Delta_i(S)=v(S\cup\{i\})-v(S)$}

A player's <marginal contribution> to a <coalition> is the increase in its value when that player joins. In a <convex cooperative game>, <marginal contributions> increase with the preceding <coalition>. Averaging contributions across player orderings gives the <Shapley value>.

= Coalition
{disambiguate=game theory}
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$S\subseteq N$}

= Coalition
{synonym}

A <coalition> is a subset of the players in a <transferable utility game>, considered as a group that can act together. The game assigns it value $v(S)$. The <core of a cooperative game> constrains the total payoff assigned to every <coalition>, while a <marginal contribution> measures the value gained by adding one player.

= Excess of a coalition
{parent=Coalition (game theory)}
{title2=$e(S,x)=v(S)-\sum_{i\in S}x_i$}

= Excesses of a coalition
{synonym}

= Coalition excess
{synonym}

The excess measures a <coalition>'s complaint against an allocation: a positive excess means its own attainable value exceeds its assigned total. The <core of a cooperative game> requires every excess to be nonpositive at an efficient allocation. The <nucleolus> lexicographically minimizes the decreasingly sorted excess vector over <imputations>.

= Convex cooperative game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S\cup T)+v(S\cap T)\ge v(S)+v(T)$}

A transferable utility game is convex when its coalition-value function is <supermodular>. Equivalently a player's <marginal contribution> cannot decrease when the preceding <coalition> grows. The implication follows by applying the displayed inequality to $A\cup\{i\}$ and $B$ with $A\subseteq B$, $i\notin B$; the reverse implication follows by adding successive marginal inequalities. This property concerns <coalition> values, not geometrical convexity of a strategy space.

= Convex simple games are unanimity games
{parent=Convex cooperative game}

Every nontrivial <simple cooperative game> that is a <convex cooperative game> is a <unanimity game>. If two <coalitions> win, the <supermodular> inequality forces their intersection to win as well. Intersecting the finite nonempty family of <winning coalitions> gives a winning <set> $K\ne\varnothing$. Every winning <coalition> contains $K$; conversely monotonicity makes every superset of $K$ win. Thus the game is $u_K$. Conversely, every <unanimity game> is a <supermodular set function>, as a direct check of the winning and losing cases shows.

= Shapley population monotonicity in a convex game
{c}
{parent=Convex cooperative game}
{title2=$\phi_i(v,N)\geq\phi_i(v,T)\quad(i\in T\subseteq N)$}

Choose a uniform random ordering of $N$ and restrict it to $T$. The restriction is uniform, and each player's predecessor set in $T$ is contained in their predecessor set in $N$. Increasing <marginal contributions> in a <convex cooperative game> make the full-population contribution at least the restricted contribution. Averaging proves the displayed <Shapley value> inequality. Summing over $i\in T$ and using efficiency in the restricted game proves every <core> inequality.

= Shapley value belongs to the core of a convex game
{c}
{parent=Convex cooperative game}

Increasing <marginal contributions> imply $m_i^\pi\ge v((S\cap P_i)\cup\{i\})-v(S\cap P_i)$ for $i\in S$. Summing telescopes to $\sum_{i\in S}m_i^\pi\ge v(S)$, while efficiency follows by telescoping over the full ordering. Hence every <marginal contribution vector> is in the <core of a cooperative game>. The <core> is a <convex set>, so their average, the <Shapley value>, is in it too. This gives an elementary proof of nonemptiness and stability for a <convex cooperative game>.

= Core
{disambiguate=game theory}
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{wiki}
{title2=$\{x:\sum_{i\in N}x_i=v(N),\quad\sum_{i\in S}x_i\ge v(S)\ \forall S\}$}

= Core
{synonym}

= Core of a cooperative game
{synonym}

The <core> consists of efficient payoff vectors for which each <coalition> receives at least its own attainable value. Thus no <coalition> can improve every member's payoff by leaving. It is a <convex set> defined by linear constraints, but can be empty. Every <convex cooperative game> has a nonempty <core> because each <marginal contribution vector> belongs to it.

= Core of a simple cooperative game
{parent=Core (game theory)}
{title2=$\operatorname{Core}(v)=\{x\ge0:\sum_i x_i=1,\ x_i=0\text{ for non-veto players}\}$}

For a monotone <simple cooperative game> with empty value $0$ and grand-<coalition> value $1$, the <core of a cooperative game> consists exactly of the nonnegative unit-total <payoff> <vector>s supported on <veto players>.

For the proof, singleton <core> conditions give $x_i\ge0$. Every <winning coalition> must receive at least $1$, the full available total, so all players outside it receive zero. A positively paid player therefore belongs to every <winning coalition> and is a <veto player>. Conversely, a unit-total nonnegative allocation supported on <veto players> gives every <winning coalition> exactly $1$ and every losing <coalition> at least $0$. These are precisely the <core> inequalities. The <core> is thus nonempty if and only if a <veto player> exists.

= Core of the miners game
{parent=Core (game theory)}
{title2=$v(S)=\lfloor|S|/r\rfloor$}

For $r\geq2$, the <core> is zero when $n<r$, the nonnegative unit simplex when $n=r$, the single allocation $x_i=1/r$ when $n\geq2r$ is divisible by $r$, and empty for all other $n\geq r$. To prove the obstruction, every $r$-person <coalition> requires payoff at least one. Summing these constraints counts each player $\binom{n-1}{r-1}$ times and gives $r\lfloor n/r\rfloor/n\geq1$, impossible when $r\nmid n$. For a divisible $n$, all such coalition sums must equal one. Comparing coalitions with the same $r-1$ members makes all individual payoffs equal when $n>r$. The uniform allocation satisfies every coalition constraint because $|S|/r\geq\lfloor|S|/r\rfloor$. At $n=r$ all proper coalition values are zero, giving the whole simplex instead.

= Shapley value
{c}
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{wiki}
{title2=$\phi_i(v)=\sum_{S\subseteq N\setminus\{i\}}\frac{|S|!(n-|S|-1)!}{n!}\bigl(v(S\cup\{i\})-v(S)\bigr)$}

The <Shapley value> averages each player's <marginal contribution> over uniformly random player orderings. Exactly $|S|!(n-|S|-1)!$ orderings have $S$ immediately before player $i$, giving the formula. The values sum to $v(N)$ by telescoping each ordering. In a <simple cooperative game> this is the <probability> of being pivotal. For a <convex cooperative game>, <Shapley value belongs to the core of a convex game> guarantees a stable allocation as well.

= Linearity of the Shapley value
{parent=Shapley value}
{title2=$\phi(av+bw)=a\phi(v)+b\phi(w)$}

The <Shapley value> is a linear map on real characteristic <functions> with zero value at the empty <coalition>. In each player <permutation>, the <marginal contribution> to $av+bw$ is $a$ times the marginal to $v$ plus $b$ times the marginal to $w$. Taking their <expectations> proves the formula for arbitrary real $a,b$. This directly proves real homogeneity as well as <cooperative-game additivity>.

= Shapley value axioms
{c}
{parent=Shapley value}

<Cooperative-game efficiency>, <cooperative-game symmetry>, the <null player> axiom and <cooperative-game additivity> characterize the <Shapley value>. The ordering-average definition satisfies all four: marginal totals telescope, relabelling players preserves the ordering average, zero marginals average to zero and marginal contributions add pointwise. The original work gives an equivalent axiomatization combining some of these requirements.<A Value for N-Person Games by Lloyd Shapley (RAND paper)>[RAND P-295, 1952, abstract: a value deduced from three axioms]{ref}

For uniqueness, write $u_T$ for the <unanimity game> of a nonempty <coalition> $T$. Recursively define $a_T=v(T)-\sum_{\varnothing\ne R\subsetneq T}a_R$ in increasing order of $|T|$. Then $v(S)=\sum_{\varnothing\ne T\subseteq S}a_T$, so $v=\sum_{\varnothing\ne T\subseteq N}a_Tu_T$. Apply the axioms to every scaled <unanimity game> $a_Tu_T$. Players outside $T$ receive zero; <cooperative-game symmetry> and <cooperative-game efficiency> give each player inside $T$ the amount $a_T/|T|$. Every game is a finite <sum> of such scaled <unanimity games>, and <cooperative-game additivity> therefore determines every allocation uniquely. Applying the axioms directly to the scaled games avoids assuming real homogeneity from additivity alone.

= Pairwise Shapley differences in a three-player simple game
{parent=Shapley value}
{title2=$\phi_i-\phi_j=\tfrac12(v(\{i\})-v(\{j\})+v(\{i,k\})-v(\{j,k\}))$}

For three distinct players $i,j,k$ in a normalized <simple cooperative game>, enumerate the predecessor <coalitions> in the <Shapley value> formula:
$$
\phi_i=\tfrac13v(\{i\})+\tfrac16\bigl(v(\{i,j\})-v(\{j\})\bigr)+\tfrac16\bigl(v(\{i,k\})-v(\{k\})\bigr)+\tfrac13\bigl(1-v(\{j,k\})\bigr).
$$
Subtracting the formula with $i$ and $j$ exchanged proves the displayed identity. Every <coalition> value is zero or one, so $2(\phi_i-\phi_j)$ is an <integer>. This rules out, for example, coordinates differing by $1/6$, although each may separately satisfy the <Shapley value denominator in a simple game>. The difference identity itself holds for arbitrary three-player <transferable utility games>; the integer restriction uses the zero-one values.

= Shapley value denominator in a simple game
{parent=Shapley value}
{title2=$n!\phi_i(v)\in\mathbb Z$}
{c}

In an $n$-player <simple cooperative game>, every <marginal contribution> along a player <permutation> is zero or one. The <Shapley value> is its average over the $n!$ equally likely <permutations>. Thus $n!\phi_i(v)$ counts the orders in which player $i$ changes a losing <coalition> into a winning one and is an <integer>. In lowest terms, the coordinate's denominator divides $n!$. This is a necessary constraint on a proposed <Shapley value> vector, not a sufficient one.

= Shapley limit in a buyer-heavy exchange market
{parent=Shapley value}
{c}
{title2=$s_k\to1,\quad b_k\to0$}

For $k$ unit sellers and $3k$ unit buyers with unit surplus per trade, a coalition's value is the smaller of its seller and buyer counts. Give each player an independent uniform arrival time. Conditional on a specified seller arriving at time $t$, the previous buyers and other sellers have independent binomial counts $B\sim\operatorname{Bin}(3k,t)$ and $S\sim\operatorname{Bin}(k-1,t)$. The seller contributes one exactly when $B>S$. For fixed positive $t$ this probability tends to one; a Chebyshev bound and a split near $t=0$ also give an explicit uniform integral estimate. Averaging gives seller <Shapley value> tending to one. Efficiency and role symmetry give $s_k+3b_k=1$, hence the buyer value tends to zero.

= Balanced contributions of Shapley values
{parent=Shapley value}
{title2=$\phi_i(N,v)-\phi_i(N\setminus\{j\},v)=\phi_j(N,v)-\phi_j(N\setminus\{i\},v)$}

For a <coalitional game>, restriction to the remaining players preserves coalition values. Remove player $j$ from a uniformly random ordering when comparing player $i$'s <Shapley value>. The marginal contribution changes only when $j$ precedes $i$. If $S$ is the set of other predecessors, the change is $v(S\cup\{i,j\})-v(S\cup\{i\})-v(S\cup\{j\})+v(S)$, with coefficient $(|S|+1)!(n-|S|-2)!/n!$. The same expression and coefficient arise after interchanging $i,j$, proving balanced contributions.

= Shapley wages in an entrepreneur-worker game
{c}
{parent=Shapley value}
{title2=$w=\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}(k+1)(p(k+1)-p(k))$}

An entrepreneur is indispensable and a <coalition> containing the entrepreneur and $k$ of $n$ workers earns $p(k)$; a <coalition> without the entrepreneur earns zero. A worker's <marginal contribution> is nonzero only when the entrepreneur precedes them. The <probability> that the entrepreneur and exactly $k$ other workers precede that worker is $(k+1)/(n(n+1))$, giving the displayed common <Shapley value> wage. The entrepreneur receives $p(n)-nw$. Convex nondecreasing $p$ makes all <marginal contributions> increase with predecessor sets, so the whole <Shapley value> allocation belongs to the <core>.

= Shapley self-duality
{parent=Shapley value}
{c}
{title2=$\phi_i(v')=\phi_i(v)$}

Complementing predecessor sets within $N\setminus\{i\}$ preserves their <Shapley value> weights, since the factorials $|S|!$ and $(n-|S|-1)!$ interchange. For the <dual coalitional game>, the corresponding <marginal contribution> equals the original contribution at that complementary set. Hence the values coincide. The same bijection gives $\phi_i(v)=\sum_S w(S)(v(N\setminus S)-v(S))$.

= Marginal contribution vector
{parent=Shapley value}
{title2=$m_i^\pi=v(P_i\cup\{i\})-v(P_i)$}

For a player ordering $\pi$, $P_i$ is the set before player $i$, and the displayed entries form its <marginal contribution> vector. Their sum is $v(N)-v(\varnothing)$. The <Shapley value> is the average of these vectors. In a <convex cooperative game>, increasing <marginal contributions> make each such vector satisfy every <coalition> constraint of the <core of a cooperative game>.

= Weighted voting game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S)=1_{\{\sum_{i\in S}w_i>t\}}$}

A weighted voting game assigns player weights and a threshold, and declares <coalitions> winning when their total weight passes that threshold. The strict $>t$ convention is equivalent to quota $t+1$ for integer weights; another common convention writes a weak inequality directly. Nontrivial examples are <simple cooperative games>. Having nonnegative weights does not guarantee a <convex cooperative game>: two-of-three majority supplies a counterexample.

= Trade obstruction to weighted voting
{parent=Weighted voting game}

Suppose winning <coalitions> $W_1,\ldots,W_k$ and losing <coalitions> $L_1,\ldots,L_k$ contain each player the same total number of times:
$$
\sum_{j=1}^{k}\mathbf1_{\{i\in W_j\}}
=\sum_{j=1}^{k}\mathbf1_{\{i\in L_j\}}
\quad\text{for every player }i.
$$
No real-weight, weak-quota <weighted voting game> represents those outcomes. Summing weights over the winning family gives at least $kq$, while summing over the losing family gives strictly less than $kq$. Equal player multiplicities make the two sums identical, a contradiction. This certificate works without assuming nonnegative weights.

For example, the winners $\{1,2,3\},\{1,4,5\}$ and losers $\{1,2,4\},\{1,3,5\}$ form a two-against-two obstruction. A monotone game whose minimal winning <coalitions> are the first two sets realizes this certificate, even though every winner contains player $1$.

= Swap robustness for disjoint winning coalitions
{parent=Weighted voting game}

A zero-one <coalitional game> has this property if exchanging one player each way between two disjoint winning <coalitions> leaves at least one winning. Any <weighted voting game> satisfies it: the exchange preserves their aggregate weight, which is at least twice the quota, whereas two losing coalitions would have aggregate weight strictly below twice the quota.

The disjointness restriction is substantive. If every winning <coalition> contains a common player, the condition is vacuous. It is consequently weaker than restrictions allowing exchanges between overlapping winning <coalitions>, and does not characterize <weighted voting games>.

= Simple cooperative game
{parent=Transferable utility game}
{title2=$v(S)\in\{0,1\}$}

A simple cooperative game is a monotone <transferable utility game> in which each <coalition> either loses, with value zero, or wins, with value one. In the usual nontrivial convention the empty <coalition> loses and the grand <coalition> wins. The <Shapley value> is then the <probability> that a player changes a losing <coalition> into a winning one when players enter in uniformly random order.

= Unanimity game
{parent=Simple cooperative game}
{title2=$u_K(S)=\mathbf1_{\{K\subseteq S\}}$}

For a fixed nonempty <coalition> $K$, the <simple cooperative game> in which a <coalition> wins exactly when it contains $K$. Members of $K$ are exactly the <veto players>. It is a <convex cooperative game>: intersections of <winning coalitions> still contain $K$, and checking the remaining zero-one cases establishes the <supermodular> inequality. Full <symmetry of players in a cooperative game> forces $K=N$.

= Veto player
{parent=Simple cooperative game}

A player belonging to every <winning coalition>. Giving all of the grand-<coalition> value to a <veto player> satisfies every <core> constraint in a nontrivial <simple cooperative game>. If there are no <veto players>, its <core of a simple cooperative game> is empty.

= Winning coalition
{parent=Simple cooperative game}
{title2=$v(S)=1$}

A <coalition> of value $1$ in a <simple cooperative game>. Monotonicity makes every superset of a <winning coalition> winning. An inclusion-minimal <winning coalition> has no winning proper <subset>.

= Payoff-equivalent strategies
{parent=Game theory}
{title2=$u(s,t)=u(s',t)\quad\text{for every }t$}

= Payoff-equivalent
{synonym}

Two strategies are payoff-equivalent for a player when they give that player the same payoff against every strategy of the opponents. If every player's payoff is also unchanged, replacing one by the other preserves the game outcomes entirely. This is distinct from <weakly dominated strategies>, whose definition requires a strict comparison somewhere.

= Normal-form game
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

A normal-form game specifies each player's strategy set and their payoff for every strategy profile. In a finite two-player game these data form a <bimatrix game>; if the two payoffs always sum to zero, one <payoff matrix> describes the <matrix game>. The representation specifies choices and incentives, without a temporal order of observed moves.

= Stag hunt
{parent=Normal-form game}
{wiki}

= Threshold stag-hunt equilibrium count
{parent=Stag hunt}
{title2=$k\leq s<n,\quad d/(s+1)\leq h\leq d/s$}

Consider $n$ players choosing a safe action worth $h$ or a joint action whose total reward $d$ is shared equally among its $s$ participants if $s\geq k$, and is zero otherwise. Assume $0<h<d$ and $2\leq k\leq n$. The safe-action profile is a <Nash equilibrium>. For $0<s<n$, a profile is a <Nash equilibrium> exactly under the displayed conditions: a joint participant must prefer $d/s$ to $h$, and a safe participant must prefer $h$ to $d/(s+1)$. An unsuccessful participant would strictly prefer the safe action. The all-joint endpoint is an equilibrium exactly when $d/n\geq h$.

Writing $r=d/h$, positive equilibrium counts lie at $s\leq r\leq s+1$ with $s\geq k$, or at $s=n$ when $r\geq n$. There are at most two. Exactly one exists when $r\geq k$ and $r\notin\{k+1,\ldots,n\}$. An integer $r$ in that excluded set permits both $r-1$ and $r$. Strict positivity matters: when $h=0<d$, the positive equilibrium counts instead include every $1\leq s\leq k-2$ as well as $n$.

= Binary game
{parent=Normal-form game}

A <binary game> is a finite <normal-form game> in which each player's <payoff> at every pure <strategy profile> is either zero or one. This describes the numerical <payoffs>, not the number of players or available actions. A <mixed strategy> can have an intermediate <expected payoff>. If a weighted average of binary <payoffs> is at least one, every positive-weight term must equal one; this observation makes pure-by-mixed domination unusually simple in <binary games>.

= Equilibrium in a two-row binary game
{parent=Binary game}

Write column $j$ of a two-row <binary game> as $((a_j,c_j),(b_j,d_j))^T$, with the row player's <payoff> first. If there is no pure <Nash equilibrium>, the column player's maximum <payoff> in each row must be one. Otherwise every column would be a <best response> in a zero-payoff row, and excluding an equilibrium there would force the opposite row to dominate in every column, giving an equilibrium in that opposite row.

A column maximizing the column player's top-row <payoff> must then have $(a_j,b_j)=(0,1)$, or its top cell would be an equilibrium. Its bottom column-player <payoff> must be zero, or its bottom cell would be one. Thus a column $((0,1),(1,0))^T$ occurs. The same argument for the bottom row produces $((1,0),(0,1))^T$. No column can give the column player <payoffs> $(1,1)$, since a row-player <best response> within that column would be a pure equilibrium.

Consequently, if neither pure equilibrium is found, mix equally over the two rows and equally over those two special columns. The row player's two <expected payoffs> are $1/2$; every column gives the column player at most $1/2$, and the supported columns achieve $1/2$. This is a <Nash equilibrium>. Computing the two row maxima, scanning for pure equilibria and locating the two special columns takes $O(n)$ <running time> for $n$ columns.

= Discrete location game
{parent=Normal-form game}

A <discrete location game> can model firms choosing sites from $\{1,\ldots,k\}$ while customers at those sites choose a nearest firm. The customer masses, distance rule, permission to share a location and rule for ties are part of the model. For two firms, customer value two per site and equal splitting of ties, the <payoff> to a firm at $x$ against one at $y$ is
$$
u(x,y)=\begin{cases}x+y-1,&x<y,\\k,&x=y,\\2k-x-y+1,&x>y.\end{cases}
$$
For $x<y$, counting sites strictly left of the midpoint and assigning half of an equidistant site's value gives $x+y-1$, whether $x+y$ is <even> or <odd>. The other firm receives the remainder, so this is a <constant-sum game> with total $2k$. Changing tie priority can fundamentally change its <Nash equilibria>, especially when co-location is permitted.

= Final entrant payoff in a discrete location game
{parent=Discrete location game}

For $k\geq3$, suppose two distinct occupied sites are $\ell<r$, a final firm must choose a different site, customers have value two per site and nearest-firm ties are split equally. A final firm left of both incumbents maximizes its <payoff> uniquely at $\ell-1$, when that site exists, obtaining $2(\ell-1)$. To the right it maximizes uniquely at $r+1$, obtaining $2(k-r)$. At every available interior site it obtains the same <payoff> $r-\ell$. Thus all <best responses> are obtained by comparing these three values, omitting regions with no available site and retaining every tied maximizing choice.

More generally, three distinct locations $a<b<c$ give their left, middle and right firms <payoffs> $a+b-1$, $c-a$ and $2k-b-c+1$. Counting the two midpoint boundaries proves these formulas; the middle firm's demand is the difference between the cumulative demands at the two boundaries. The formulas retain all even/odd midpoint ties automatically.

= Strategy profile
{parent=Normal-form game}
{title2=$s=(s_1,\ldots,s_n)$}

A <strategy profile> specifies one strategy for every player. A <pure-strategy> <strategy profile> lies in the <Cartesian product> $S_1\times\cdots\times S_n$ of the players' strategy <sets>. A <mixed-strategy> <strategy profile> specifies one <probability distribution> $\sigma_i$ over each $S_i$. Write $s_{-i}$ or $\sigma_{-i}$ for the choices of all players except player $i$.

= Unilateral deviation
{parent=Normal-form game}
{title2=$(s_i,s_{-i})\mapsto(t_i,s_{-i})$}

A <unilateral deviation> changes only one player's strategy, holding the other players' strategies fixed. It is profitable if it strictly increases that player's <payoff>, or <expected payoff> for <mixed strategies>. The comparison does not allow the opponents to adjust in response to the deviation. In a sequential game, however, holding an opponent's complete <pure strategy> fixed still allows their prescribed actions to differ after different observed histories.

= Subgame perfect equilibrium
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

= Subgame perfect equilibria
{synonym}

A strategy profile that is a <Nash equilibrium> in every subgame, including those outside the realized play path. In finite sequential games, <backward induction> constructs such profiles by solving continuation games before earlier decisions. When a stage is an <all-pay contest>, its solution can involve <mixed strategies>.

= Stackelberg competition
{parent=Game theory}
{c}
{wiki}

A sequential model in <game theory> in which a leader commits to an action before a follower observes it and chooses a <best response>. The leader anticipates the follower's response when optimizing. With private follower information, the leader maximizes its expected payoff over follower types. The resulting solution is a <Stackelberg equilibrium>.

= Stackelberg equilibrium
{parent=Stackelberg competition}
{c}

A solution of <Stackelberg competition> in which the follower chooses a <best response> after each observed leader action and the leader optimizes given those responses. Indifference may require a specified response or leader-selection convention; it cannot be silently treated as a strict inequality. In a <sequential private-value all-pay contest>, the leader's objective is $vF(b)-b$.

= Contest theory
{parent=Game theory}

Contest theory studies how <prize allocation rules> and effort costs influence strategic effort investment, participation, and the resulting <Nash equilibria> or <Bayesian Nash equilibria>. Unlike a sale in which only the winner pays, an <all-pay auction> charges every participant for its effort.

= Sequential elimination all-pay contest
{parent=Contest theory}

A finite sequence of <all-pay contests> in which each stage awards one prize and its winner leaves. Losing players remain eligible for later stages. A player can receive at most one prize, while its effort costs are incurred whenever it participates. <Subgame perfect equilibrium> accounts for both the immediate prize and the value of remaining eligible.

= Backward-induction threshold in an elimination all-pay contest
{parent=Sequential elimination all-pay contest}
{title2=$C_r=(1-\delta)\sum_{j=2}^{r}\delta^{j-2}v_j+\delta^{r-1}v_{r+1}$}

With ordered values $v_1>\cdots>v_k$ and $r<k$ prizes remaining, define $C_r=(1-\delta)\sum_{j=2}^{r}\delta^{j-2}v_j+\delta^{r-1}v_{r+1}$. In the recursively constructed discounted <subgame perfect equilibrium>, it is the second active player's effective prize and the effort-support upper endpoint. The highest player's utility is $v_1-C_r$. The coefficients form a convex combination, so $C_r\geq v_{r+1}$, allowing lower-player deviations to be bounded.

= Vanishing-discount limit of an elimination all-pay contest
{parent=Backward-induction threshold in an elimination all-pay contest}

As $\delta\uparrow1$, both active players' effective prizes in every nonfinal subgame tend to the marginal valuation $v_{r+1}$. The <two-player complete-information all-pay equilibrium> then gives each a winning probability $1/2$. In the final stage, actual valuations determine the asymmetric winning probabilities. Taking this limit from discounted equilibria specifies the continuation selection instead of independently choosing an undiscounted game equilibrium.

= Ranked winning probabilities in an undiscounted elimination contest
{parent=Vanishing-discount limit of an elimination all-pay contest}
{title2=$x_i=1-2^{-(m-i+2)}v_{m+1}/v_i\quad(2\leq i\leq m)$}

For $m<n$ prizes and ordered distinct valuations, the discounted-equilibrium limit gives $x_1=1-2^{-m}v_{m+1}/v_1$ and $x_i=1-2^{-(m-i+2)}v_{m+1}/v_i$ for $2\leq i\leq m$. A player must lose its successive fair nonfinal contests and then the asymmetric final contest to receive no prize. The marginal player's probability is $m-\sum_{i=1}^m x_i$, and lower players have probability zero.

= Discounted continuation value in an elimination contest
{parent=Sequential elimination all-pay contest}
{title2=$\delta J_i(S\setminus\{j\},r-1)$}

If $J_i(S,r)$ is a player's net expected utility with remaining players $S$ and $r$ prizes, losing the current stage to player $j$ gives baseline $\delta J_i(S\setminus\{j\},r-1)$. This baseline includes later prize values and later effort costs. Subtracting it from the immediate prize value gives an <effective prize in a sequential contest>. Discounting must be applied to utilities, rather than to winning probabilities.

= Effective prize in a sequential contest
{parent=Discounted continuation value in an elimination contest}
{title2=$A_i=v_i-\delta J_i^{\mathrm{lose}}$}

Relative to losing against a particular rival, the gain from winning now is the current value minus discounted continuation utility. In an elimination contest this is $A_i=v_i-\delta J_i(S\setminus\{j\},r-1)$. When the relevant losing baseline is fixed, current effort incentives reduce to a <two-player complete-information all-pay equilibrium>. In general, the baseline can depend on which rival wins; that dependence must be verified before using a single effective prize.

= Sequential private-value all-pay contest
{parent=Contest theory}

Two players pay their invested efforts regardless of outcome. A leader invests first, and a follower observes that effort before deciding. With ties favoring the follower and unit costs, a follower of value $u$ matches leader effort $b$ when $u>b$, and otherwise declines. For a continuous follower <cumulative distribution function> $F$, the leader wins with probability $F(b)$ and has payoff $vF(b)-b$.

= Ex ante follower advantage in a sequential all-pay contest
{parent=Sequential private-value all-pay contest}
{title2=$\mathbb P(\text{leader wins})\leq1/2$}

Every optimal leader bid satisfies $b(v)\leq v$, because bidding zero guarantees payoff zero and winning probability is at most one. Hence $F(b(v))\leq F(v)$. With identically distributed independent continuous valuations, the <probability integral transform> gives $\mathbb E F(V)=1/2$. The leader's unconditional winning probability is therefore at most $1/2$, even when some high leader types have a conditional advantage. This argument does not depend on selecting a unique optimum.

= Leader optimization in a sequential private-value all-pay contest
{parent=Sequential private-value all-pay contest}
{title2=$b(v)\in\arg\max_b\{vF(b)-b\}$}

With follower distribution $F$, a leader of value $v$ chooses $b\in\arg\max_{0\leq b\leq1}\{vF(b)-b\}$. If $F$ is a <concave function>, the objective is concave and an interior optimum satisfies $vF'(b)=1$. Endpoint derivatives handle zero or maximal effort. Zero effort guarantees nonnegative payoff, so every optimum satisfies $b\leq vF(b)\leq v$.

= Best-response selection at a flat leader objective
{parent=Leader optimization in a sequential private-value all-pay contest}

If a concave leader objective $vF(b)-b$ is constant over an interval, all efforts there are <best responses>, but they can have different winning probabilities. A flat positive-density interval of $F$ produces this at the type $v=1/F'$. Thus strict threshold statements must distinguish strict concavity from a selection such as the smallest maximizing effort. For an atom-free type distribution, the specified threshold type has zero ex ante probability.

= Median-density threshold for the leader in an all-pay contest
{parent=Leader optimization in a sequential private-value all-pay contest}
{title2=$v>1/F'(F^{-1}(1/2))$}

For increasing concave $F$, write $q=F^{-1}(1/2)$. Under the smallest-maximizer convention, the leader wins with probability greater than $1/2$ exactly when $v>1/F'(q)$. The sign of $vF'(q)-1$ places the maximizer before or after $q$. At equality, concavity alone permits a flat interval of optima; strict concavity or an explicit selection is needed for the strict comparison at that type.

= Proportional allocation contest
{parent=Contest theory}
{title2=$x_i(b)=b_i/\sum_j b_j$}

For positive total effort, a proportional allocation contest awards player $i$ the prize with <probability> $b_i/\sum_j b_j$, or gives it that proportion of a divisible prize. A convention is needed at zero total effort. This allocation is different from the discontinuous highest-effort rule of an <all-pay auction>.

= Proportional contest with outside effort
{parent=Proportional allocation contest}
{title2=$x_i=b_i/(R+\delta)$}

An <all-pay contest> with allocations $x_i=b_i/(\sum_jb_j+\delta)$ includes fixed outside effort $\delta\geq0$. A player with unit effort cost has payoff $v_ix_i-b_i$. The outside effort represents an ineligible fixed competitor and may leave some prize probability unallocated to eligible players. At equilibrium, a player is active exactly when its value exceeds total eligible effort plus outside effort.

= Active-set threshold for a proportional contest with outside effort
{parent=Proportional contest with outside effort}
{title2=$\delta<v_j(2-j+\sum_{i<j}v_j/v_i)$}

For ordered positive valuations, rank $j$ is active exactly when $\delta<v_j(2-j+\sum_{i<j}v_j/v_i)$. These ranks form a prefix, and the number active is the largest qualifying rank, or zero if none qualifies. This follows by evaluating the decreasing equilibrium equation $\sum_i(1-S/v_i)_+-1+\delta/S=0$ at $S=v_j$. Strict inequality excludes zero-effort boundary players.

= Total-effort formula for a proportional contest with outside effort
{parent=Proportional contest with outside effort}
{title2=$H_k(R+\delta)^2-(k-1)(R+\delta)-\delta=0$}

With $k>0$ active players of <harmonic mean> valuation $\bar v_k$, unit-cost <Nash equilibrium> effort satisfies $R+\delta=\frac{\bar v_k}{2k}[(k-1)+\sqrt{(k-1)^2+4k\delta/\bar v_k}]$. Sum the active-player first-order equations $b_i=(R+\delta)(1-(R+\delta)/v_i)$ to obtain the quadratic. If $\delta$ is at least the largest valuation, no one is active and $R=0$. With no outside effort, at least two players must be active.

= Quadratic-cost two-player proportional contest
{parent=Proportional allocation contest}
{title2=$b_i=\dfrac{\sqrt{v_i}}{\sqrt{v_1}+\sqrt{v_2}}\,\dfrac{(v_1v_2)^{1/4}}{\sqrt2}$}

With values $v_i>0$ and effort costs $b_i^2$, the unique pure <Nash equilibrium> has the efforts displayed above. Interior <first-order conditions> give $v_i b_j/(b_1+b_2)^2=2b_i$, hence $b_1/b_2=\sqrt{v_1/v_2}$ and $(b_1+b_2)^2=\sqrt{v_1v_2}/2$. Against a positive rival effort, each payoff is a <strictly concave function>, so these conditions identify global <best responses>. On the axes, lowering a positive uncontested effort or adding a sufficiently small effort at the zero tie rules out equilibrium.

= Simultaneous all-pay contests
{parent=Contest theory}

Players choose efforts in several <all-pay contests> at the same time. With additive <quasilinear utility> and no shared effort budget, a player's expected payoff is the sum of its per-contest payoffs. An entry restriction couples the choices of contests even though the subsequent effort optimization is separable.

= Two-of-three all-pay participation equilibrium
{parent=Simultaneous all-pay contests}
{title2=$q_j=\dfrac{w_j^{-1/(n-1)}}{\sum_{\ell=1}^3w_\ell^{-1/(n-1)}}$}

Consider $n\geq2$ identical players, prizes $w_j>0$, unit effort costs, and exactly two entries per player. The symmetric equilibrium omission probabilities $q_j$ sum to one. The common per-contest payoff is $u=(\sum_j w_j^{-1/(n-1)})^{-(n-1)}$, and the unconditional rival distribution on $0\leq b\leq w_j-u$ is $G_j(b)=((b+u)/w_j)^{1/(n-1)}$. Conditional on entry, its <distribution function> is $(G_j(b)-q_j)/(1-q_j)$. Choosing the omitted contest according to $q$ and sampling the two active efforts independently gives a <symmetric equilibrium> with expected payoff $2u$. These formulas determine the marginal laws; they do not determine the dependence between a player's two efforts.

= Marginal-equivalent equilibria in additive contests
{parent=Two-of-three all-pay participation equilibrium}

If contest rewards and costs add, a fixed deviation's expected payoff depends on each rival's per-contest <marginal distributions>, rather than on dependence between that rival's efforts across contests. Replacing the conditional independent sampling of two active efforts by another <copula> with the same conditional marginals therefore preserves every deviation payoff. Consequently it preserves the <Nash equilibrium>, even when the new joint strategy distribution is different. This explains why unique participation probabilities and bid marginals need not imply a unique full <mixed strategy>.

= All-pay indifference equation with random entry
{parent=Simultaneous all-pay contests}
{title2=$wG(b)^{n-1}-b=u$}

For a prize of value $w$, let $G(b)$ be the probability that a rival is absent or enters with effort at most $b$. When rivals act independently and have no atoms at positive efforts, effort $b>0$ wins with probability $G(b)^{n-1}$. If a player mixes over an interval of <best responses>, its payoff on that interval is constant, so $G(b)=((b+u)/w)^{1/(n-1)}$. If the absence probability is $q$ and the active <effort support> starts at zero, then $u=wq^{n-1}$.

= Rank-order contest
{parent=Contest theory}

A rank-order contest awards a sequence of prizes according to the ranks of efforts, while every player incurs its own effort cost. With an <independent private values model>, an increasing symmetric bidding function orders efforts in the same way as valuations.

= All-pay effort identity
{parent=Rank-order contest}
{title2=$b(v)=\int_{\underline v}^v t a'(t)dt$}

Suppose values are nonnegative, the <rank-order expected prize allocation> $a$ is increasing and differentiable, and $a(\underline v)=0$. With unit effort cost and zero effort at the lowest type, the symmetric equilibrium effort is $b(v)=v a(v)-\int_{\underline v}^v a(t)dt$. A true type $v$ imitating type $z$ receives utility $v a(z)-b(z)$, whose derivative in $z$ is $(v-z)a'(z)$. It increases up to $v$ and decreases afterwards, proving the <best response> property. This is the <interim payment identity> specialized to an <all-pay contest>.

= Expected effort in a rank-order contest
{parent=All-pay effort identity}
{title2=$\mathbb E\sum_i b(V_i)=\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} k(w_k-w_{k+1})\mathbb E V_{[k+1]}$}

Let $V_{[1]}\geq\cdots\geq V_{[n]}$ be descending <order statistics>, with $w_n=0$ and nonnegative decreasing prizes. Decompose the prize vector into awards of $w_k-w_{k+1}$ to each of the best $k$ players. The corresponding truthful multi-unit auction charges each winner the next value $V_{[k+1]}$. Its total payment is $k(w_k-w_{k+1})V_{[k+1]}$. <Revenue equivalence> transfers the expected payment to the <all-pay contest>, because the interim allocations and lowest-type utilities coincide. Summing the layers proves the formula.

= Uniform-value multi-prize all-pay effort formula
{parent=Expected effort in a rank-order contest}
{title2=$R_m=\frac{m(n-m)}{n+1}w(m/n)$}

With $n$ independent uniform types, $m<n$ equal prizes of scale $w(m/n)$ and unit effort costs, a symmetric <Bayesian Nash equilibrium> has total expected effort $R_m=m(n-m)w(m/n)/(n+1)$. The <all-pay effort identity> yields $b(v)=w(m/n)\int_0^v t q_m'(t)dt$. The allocation derivative is a <Beta distribution> density with parameters $n-m,m$, so integration gives the formula.

= Discrete prize-count optimization for a power-valued contest
{parent=Uniform-value multi-prize all-pay effort formula}
{title2=$x_*=(1-\alpha)/(2-\alpha)$}

For prize scale $w(x)=x^{-\alpha}$, total effort is proportional to $h(x)=x^{1-\alpha}(1-x)$ on the feasible grid $x=m/n$. If $\alpha\geq1$, one prize is optimal. For $0<\alpha<1$, $h$ increases up to $x_*=(1-\alpha)/(2-\alpha)$ and then decreases, so the optimal feasible integer is among $\lfloor nx_*\rfloor$ and $\lfloor nx_*\rfloor+1$. Remove infeasible candidates and compare their objective values. No definition at $x=0$ is needed.

= Rank-order expected prize allocation
{parent=Rank-order contest}
{title2=$a(v)=\sum_{k=1}^n w_k\binom{n-1}{k-1}(1-F(v))^{k-1}F(v)^{n-k}$}

For $n$ players with independent valuations having a continuous <distribution function> $F$, a type $v$ occupies rank $k$ when exactly $k-1$ rivals have higher values. Multiplying this <binomial distribution> by the rank prize $w_k$ and summing gives $a(v)$. Ties occur only on <zero-probability events> when the valuation law is atomless.

= Prize allocation rule
{parent=Contest theory}
{title2=$x_i(b)\geq0,\quad\sum_i x_i(b)\leq1$}

A prize allocation rule assigns winning probabilities or divisible prize shares to a profile of efforts. In an <all-pay auction>, the greatest effort wins; a <proportional allocation contest> instead assigns shares continuously according to relative effort. The rule is essential data of a contest model.

= Winning probability
{parent=Prize allocation rule}
{title2=$x_i(b)$}

A winning probability is the <probability> that a specified participant receives the prize, conditional on the submitted efforts and the allocation rule's randomization.

= Bayesian game
{c}
{parent=Game theory}

A Bayesian game gives players private information about their types, a probability model for that information, and strategies depending on the player's own type. A <private-value auction> with uncertain other valuations is a Bayesian game.

= Bayesian Nash equilibrium
{c}
{parent=Bayesian game}
{title2=$s_i(\theta_i)$}

= Bayes-Nash equilibrium
{c}
{synonym}

= Bayesian Nash equilibria
{c}
{synonym}

A Bayesian Nash equilibrium consists of type-dependent strategies such that every type maximizes its conditional expected payoff given the other players' strategies and the type distribution. It is a <Nash equilibrium> in the strategic description whose actions are whole contingent strategy functions. Auction incentive comparisons can test a deviation by imitating another type's equilibrium bid.

= Mechanism design
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

Mechanism design chooses allocation and payment rules while accounting for how agents' information and incentives affect their reports. <Social choice theory> studies collective choice from preferences, while <auction> design also uses monetary transfers.

= Allocation externality
{parent=Mechanism design}

Dependence of a participant's <utility> on assignments made to other participants, beyond the identity of that participant's own assigned object. In <room allocation with friendship externalities>, the same room can become more valuable when a friend receives an adjacent room. An early participant in a sequential allocation must therefore anticipate later choices.

= Serial dictatorship
{parent=Mechanism design}

An allocation procedure using a fixed priority order: each participant in turn selects one remaining object, which is permanently assigned to them. For participants whose <utilities> depend only on their own assigned objects, strict rankings give a unique best available choice and the resulting allocation is <Pareto optimal>. If a proposed <Pareto improvement> changes an assignment, its earliest changed participant loses their unique best available object. With ties or <allocation externalities>, that argument requires additional assumptions.

<One-sided matchings: house allocation problems in the Cornell networks and markets text>[§13.2, serial dictator mechanism and Theorem 13.2]{ref}

= Room allocation with friendship externalities
{parent=Serial dictatorship}
{title2=$u_i(a)=v_{i,a_i}+f_i\mathbf1_{\{|a_i-a_{F(i)}|=1\}}$}

Assign distinct rooms along a hallway to participants. A participant's <utility> is their own room value plus a positive bonus if their designated friend receives a physically adjacent room; participants without friends receive only their room value. When friendships form disjoint pairs, consecutive choosing slots allow each pair to settle its own adjacency before later participants act. Consecutive slots in <serial dictatorship> do not themselves require adjacent allocated rooms.

= Pareto efficiency of serial dictatorship with consecutive friends
{parent=Room allocation with friendship externalities}
{c}

Suppose each participant has at most one mutual friend, each friendship pair chooses consecutively, room values lie in $[0,1]$ and are distinct for each participant, and each friendship bonus exceeds $1$. Then every <subgame perfect equilibrium> outcome of <serial dictatorship> is <Pareto optimal>.

For a friend pair, if any remaining adjacent room pair exists, the leader chooses their uniquely best room having an available neighbor; the follower chooses their uniquely best available neighbor. Adjacency dominates every possible difference in own-room value for both friends. If no adjacent room pair remains, neither friend can obtain a bonus and each chooses their unique best available room. A participant without a friend also chooses their unique best available room.

For the proof, compare a purported <Pareto improvement> block by block, where a block is a single participant without a friend or one consecutive friendship pair. Earlier fixed blocks leave the same rooms available. A block receiving adjacency cannot lose it in a weak improvement. Its leader must retain the uniquely best eligible room, and its follower must retain the uniquely best neighboring room. Without available adjacency, both rooms are fixed by strict own-room rankings. <Mathematical induction> fixes every assignment, excluding a strict improvement.

= Expected seller revenue
{parent=Mechanism design}
{title2=$\mathbb E[R]$}

= Expected revenue
{synonym}

The expectation of the seller's total receipts over the buyers' valuation types and any randomization in the sales mechanism. In an <auction> it is the sum of the bidders' expected payments; with a <posted price> it is price times sale probability. It does not subtract selling costs or the seller's opportunity cost of retaining the item, so it is revenue rather than profit.

= Posted price
{parent=Mechanism design}
{title2=$p$}

A seller commits to a fixed price and sells if at least one buyer accepts. With two independent valuations having common cumulative distribution $F$, the sale probability is $1-F(p)^2$ and expected revenue is $p(1-F(p)^2)$. The winner's selection among multiple willing buyers does not affect this revenue.

= Mechanism
{disambiguate=mechanism design}
{parent=Mechanism design}

= Mechanism
{synonym}

A mechanism specifies participants' available messages, an allocation rule, and any payment rule. <Mechanism design> chooses these rules to achieve an objective while respecting participants' <incentive compatibility> and <individual rationality>.

= Single-parameter mechanism
{parent=Mechanism design}

In a single-parameter mechanism, player $i$ has one private scalar value $v_i$, receives an allocation amount $x_i$, and has <quasilinear utility> $v_i x_i-p_i$. The allocation-payment relation is governed by <incentive compatibility> and the <interim payment identity>.

= Virtual valuation
{parent=Single-parameter mechanism}
{wiki}
{title2=$\phi(v)=v-\dfrac{1-F(v)}{f(v)}$}

For an absolutely continuous valuation distribution with density $f(v)>0$, its virtual valuation is $v-(1-F(v))/f(v)$. The <virtual-surplus revenue identity> converts expected incentive-compatible payments into expected allocations weighted by virtual valuations. A <regular prior> makes this quantity nondecreasing.

= Virtual surplus
{parent=Virtual valuation}
{title2=$\sum_i\phi_i(v_i)x_i(v)$}

Virtual surplus weights allocation amounts by the corresponding <virtual valuations>. Under the hypotheses of the <virtual-surplus revenue identity>, maximizing it subject to implementability and feasibility yields a revenue-optimal mechanism when lowest-type utilities can be normalized to zero.

= Virtual-surplus revenue identity
{parent=Virtual valuation}
{title2=$\mathbb E\sum_i P_i(V_i)=\mathbb E\sum_i\phi_i(V_i)X_i(V_i)-\sum_i U_i(\underline v_i)$}

For an <independent private values model>, let $X_i(v)$ and $P_i(v)$ be interim allocation and payment. The <interim payment identity> gives $P_i(v)=vX_i(v)-U_i(\underline v_i)-\int_{\underline v_i}^vX_i(t)dt$. Applying <Fubini's theorem> to reverse the order of integration yields $\mathbb E\int_{\underline v_i}^{V_i}X_i(t)dt=\int X_i(t)(1-F_i(t))dt$. Subtracting this term from $\int vX_i(v)f_i(v)dv$ proves the identity. <Interim individual rationality> bounds the lowest-type utilities below by zero.

= Revenue-optimal public-project auction
{parent=Virtual-surplus revenue identity}
{title2=$x(v)=\mathbf1_{\{\sum_i\phi_i(v_i)\geq0\}}$}

In a public-project <single-parameter mechanism>, all players receive the same binary allocation. For independent <regular priors> and voluntary participation with zero outside utility, maximizing <virtual surplus> means providing the project exactly when $\sum_i\phi_i(v_i)\geq0$. The allocation is monotone in each value, so <critical-value payments> implement it with <dominant-strategy incentive compatibility> and <ex post individual rationality>. For independent uniform values on $[0,1]$, the condition is $\sum_i v_i\geq n/2$, with winning payment $\max\{0,n/2-\sum_{j\ne i}v_j\}$.

= Regular distribution
{disambiguate=economics}
{parent=Virtual valuation}
{wiki}

= Regular prior
{synonym}

A valuation distribution is regular when its <virtual valuation> is a <nondecreasing function> of value. This is an auction-theory condition, not the unrelated regularity notions used elsewhere in mathematics.

= Direct revelation mechanism
{parent=Mechanism design}

A direct revelation mechanism asks agents to report their types directly, then computes allocation and payments from those reports. Being direct does not itself imply <strategyproofness>; the allocation and payment rules must supply the incentive guarantee.

= Revelation principle
{parent=Direct revelation mechanism}
{wiki}

An equilibrium outcome of a <mechanism> can be reproduced by asking for types and then sending the messages prescribed by the original equilibrium strategies. Truthful reports are then a <Bayesian Nash equilibrium>: a profitable false report would induce a profitable original deviation. This reduces optimization over indirect mechanisms to <direct revelation mechanisms> with <Bayesian incentive compatibility>, under the same information and participation assumptions.

= Vickrey-Clarke-Groves mechanism
{c}
{parent=Direct revelation mechanism}

= VCG mechanism
{c}
{synonym}

= Clarke pivot mechanism
{c}
{synonym}

The pivot form chooses a reported-welfare-maximizing allocation and charges each agent the maximum welfare achievable by the others without that agent minus the others' welfare in the chosen allocation. With <quasilinear utility>, truthful reporting is a dominant strategy because the first term in the payment depends only on other reports. For two identical items and three unit-demand bidders, each winner pays the lowest reported valuation and the loser pays zero.

= Incentive compatibility
{parent=Mechanism design}

Incentive compatibility makes the prescribed truthful or type-dependent strategy optimal under the specified solution concept. Dominant-strategy incentive compatibility holds for every other report profile; Bayesian incentive compatibility compares expected utilities over the other agents' types.

= Dominant-strategy incentive compatibility
{parent=Incentive compatibility}

= DSIC
{c}
{synonym}

Dominant-strategy incentive compatibility means truthful reporting maximizes a player's utility for every fixed profile of the other reports. It is the monetary-mechanism version of <strategyproofness> and implies <Bayesian incentive compatibility> under any independent prior.

= Critical-value payment
{parent=Dominant-strategy incentive compatibility}
{title2=$p_i(v)=v_i x_i(v)-\int_{\underline v_i}^{v_i}x_i(t,v_{-i})dt$}

A monotone binary allocation is made truthful by charging its winning threshold, truncated below at the lowest allowed value, and charging zero to losers. The displayed integral formula sets the lowest type's utility to zero. To check it, hold the other reports fixed: a type above the threshold benefits from winning at that price, while a type below it benefits from losing. At a threshold, either deterministic tie choice is compatible with indifference.

= Bayesian incentive compatibility
{parent=Incentive compatibility}
{c}
{title2=$vX_i(v)-P_i(v)\geq vX_i(z)-P_i(z)$}

Truthful reporting is Bayesian incentive compatible when every type maximizes its expected utility over the other players' types by reporting truthfully. In an <independent private values model>, the displayed inequality uses the same interim allocation and payment functions for every possible true type.

= Individual rationality
{parent=Mechanism design}
{title2=$u_i\geq0$}

Individual rationality means participation yields at least the outside-option utility, normalized here to zero. A risk-neutral zero-value bidder with nonnegative payments and no positive-valued allocation must have zero expected payment and utility if participation is individually rational.

= Ex post individual rationality
{parent=Individual rationality}

Ex post individual rationality requires nonnegative participation utility at every realized profile of types. A truthful threshold allocation with its <critical-value payment> satisfies this condition for nonnegative valuations.

= Interim individual rationality
{parent=Individual rationality}
{title2=$U_i(v_i)\geq0$}

Interim individual rationality requires a nonnegative expected participation utility for each private type, averaging over the other types with the player's conditional beliefs. It differs from requiring nonnegative utility at each realized profile, which is <ex post individual rationality>.

= Auction
{parent=Mechanism design}

An auction allocates goods using submitted messages and a specified payment rule. <Private-value auctions> model each bidder's valuation as its own private information. Equilibrium bids depend on the allocation and payment rules, not only on valuations.

= Vickrey auction
{parent=Auction}
{wiki}

= Second-price sealed-bid auction
{synonym}

A single-item <auction> in which the highest bidder wins and pays the highest competing bid. Under private values and <quasilinear utility>, bidding one's valuation is a <dominant strategy>: if the competing maximum is below one's value, winning at that price is best; if it is above one's value, losing is best; equality gives zero utility either way. Thus truthful bidding is an ex post <Nash equilibrium>.

= Lowest-price auction
{parent=Auction}

The highest bidder wins a single item but pays the lowest submitted bid, while losers pay nothing. This differs from a <lowest unique bid auction>. With at least two bidders using increasing strategies, the winning bidder pays the smallest opponent bid.

// Target: mathematical-optimization.bigb

= Uniform lowest-price auction equilibrium
{parent=Lowest-price auction}
{title2=$b(v)=(n-1)v$}

For $n\geq2$ risk-neutral bidders with independent <uniform distributions> on $[0,1]$, the <lowest-price auction> has a symmetric <Bayesian Nash equilibrium> $b(v)=(n-1)v$. A deviation bidding $(n-1)z$, with $0\leq z\leq1$, wins with probability $z^{n-1}$ and has expected payment $(n-1)z^n/n$. Its expected profit is $vz^{n-1}-(n-1)z^n/n$, whose derivative is $(n-1)z^{n-2}(v-z)$, proving optimality at $z=v$. Bids above $n-1$ give the same outcome as $z=1$. The seller receives $(n-1)$ times the minimum of $n$ independent uniform valuations, with expected revenue $(n-1)/(n+1)$.

// Target: mathematical-optimization.bigb

= Reserve price
{parent=Auction}
{title2=$r$}

A reserve price is the seller's minimum acceptable price. In an <English auction> it changes both the sale rule and the payment rule: the highest valuation must exceed the reserve and the winner pays at least the reserve. Mechanisms with different reserves generally have different allocation probabilities, so <revenue equivalence> need not equate their expected revenues.

= English auction
{c}
{parent=Auction}
{wiki}

= Ascending-price auction
{synonym}

In an ideal continuous-price auction with independent private values, bidders remain active up to their valuations. Without a reserve, the last remaining bidder wins at the second-highest valuation. With a <reserve price> $r$, there is no sale if every valuation is below $r$; otherwise the winner pays the larger of $r$ and the second-highest valuation. Strict versus weak inequalities at the reserve have no revenue effect for continuous valuation distributions. This payment rule is distinct from a <first-price sealed-bid auction>.

= Least unique bid auction
{parent=Auction}

= Lowest unique bid auction
{synonym}

Each bidder chooses a permitted amount. The bidder with the smallest amount submitted by exactly one bidder wins and pays that amount; if no amount is unique, nobody wins. With object value $V$, the winner's payoff is $V$ minus their bid, and losers pay nothing. Unlike a <first-price sealed-bid auction>, repeated lowest bids can be disqualified.

= Two-bid three-player least unique bid auction
{parent=Least unique bid auction}
{title2=$p_* =\dfrac{\sqrt{V-1}}{\sqrt{V-1}+\sqrt{V-2}}$}

For bids $1,2$ and $V>2$, write $p_i$ for player $i$'s <probability> of bidding $1$. The two expected payoffs are $(V-1)(1-p_j)(1-p_k)$ and $(V-2)p_jp_k$. Their unique <symmetric equilibrium> uses the displayed probability. Every permutation of $(1,0,t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$, is also a <Nash equilibrium>: the mixed bidder is indifferent, while the two pure bidders weakly prefer their prescribed bids. There are no further equilibria. Three interior probabilities must all coincide, since subtracting two indifference equations gives a nonzero positive factor times their difference. Two interior probabilities with a pure opponent are impossible, and a single interior probability requires the other two bids to differ. Thus the nonsymmetric equilibria form six line segments with six pure endpoints, and their number is uncountable.

= All-pay auction
{parent=Auction}
{wiki}

= All-pay contest
{synonym}

In a standard all-pay auction, every player pays its bid or effort cost, and the highest bid receives the prize. With a value $v_i$, unit effort cost, and <winning probability> $x_i(b)$, player $i$ has <quasilinear utility> $v_i x_i(b)-b_i$. Equal highest bids require an explicit tie rule.

= Two-player complete-information all-pay equilibrium
{parent=All-pay auction}
{title2=$p_1=1-A_2/(2A_1),\quad p_2=A_2/(2A_1)$}

For effective prizes $A_1\geq A_2>0$ and unit effort costs, equilibrium effort CDFs on $[0,A_2]$ are $G_1(b)=b/A_2$ and $G_2(b)=1-A_2/A_1+b/A_1$. The weaker player has an atom at zero. Incremental utilities are $A_1-A_2$ and zero, and winning probabilities are $1-A_2/(2A_1)$ and $A_2/(2A_1)$. Direct payoff indifference and exclusion of larger bids verify the <Nash equilibrium>. A further player with effective prize at most $A_2$ cannot profit by entering against these distributions.

= First-price sealed-bid auction
{parent=Auction}

Each bidder submits one private bid; the highest bidder wins and pays its own bid, while losers pay nothing. A multi-unit version can award one item to each of the highest bidders, each paying its own bid. In a monotone symmetric equilibrium, a bidder can imitate another type's bid when checking incentive constraints.

= No dominant positive-value bid in a first-price auction
{parent=First-price sealed-bid auction}
{title2=$v>0$}

In a <first-price sealed-bid auction> with at least two bidders, continuous nonnegative bids, <quasilinear utility> and positive private value $v$, no bid is a <dominant strategy>. Against opponents all bidding zero, any positive bid can be lowered while still winning, increasing the payoff. Bid zero fails against an opposing bid $v/2$, since bidding $3v/4$ earns $v/4>0$. Nor does randomizing create a <dominant strategy>. Against all-zero opponents, a random bid has expected payoff strictly below $v$ unless it is identically zero and receives the item with certainty under the tie rule. In the strict case, a sufficiently small positive fixed bid earns more. In the exceptional case, the deviation against $v/2$ again improves the payoff. Finite expected utility is implicit; an expected payoff of negative infinity cannot dominate finite-payoff bids. The positive-value condition matters: a zero-value bidder weakly prefers bid zero.

= Uniform private-value first-price bidding equilibrium
{parent=First-price sealed-bid auction}
{title2=$\beta(v)=\frac{n-1}{n}v$}

For $n\geq2$ <risk-neutral> bidders with independent uniform valuations on $[0,1]$, this rule is a symmetric <Bayesian Nash equilibrium>. If opponents use $\beta(v)=av$, a bid $b\in[0,a]$ has expected utility $(v-b)(b/a)^{n-1}$. Its derivative has the sign of $(n-1)v-nb$, so with $a=(n-1)/n$ the best bid is $av$. Bids above $a$ guarantee winning but only increase payment. The type-$v$ equilibrium utility is $v^n/n$.

= Interim payment identity
{parent=Auction}
{title2=$m(\theta)=\theta G(\theta)-\int_0^\theta G(t)dt-u(0)$}

For a risk-neutral single-parameter bidder with incentive-compatible type reports, interim utility satisfies $u'(\theta)=G(\theta)$ wherever the winning probability is continuous. Consequently expected payment is determined by allocation probabilities and the utility of the lowest type. The common normalization $u(0)=0$ must be justified, not obtained from allocation alone.

= Revenue equivalence
{parent=Interim payment identity}

= Revenue equivalence theorem
{synonym}

Two auctions with the same interim allocation probabilities and the same lowest-type utilities have the same interim expected payments under the usual risk-neutral single-parameter incentive conditions. Equal realized payments are not required. This conclusion follows directly from the <interim payment identity>.

= Private-value auction
{parent=Auction}

A private-value auction gives each bidder its own value for receiving an item; the value is determined by its own type rather than by another bidder's information. An <independent private values model> also assumes independence between types.

= Unit-demand valuation
{parent=Private-value auction}
{title2=$v_i(a,\theta_i)=\theta_i\mathbf1_{\{i\in a\}}$}

A bidder with unit demand values receiving one item but obtains no additional value from extra identical units. Selecting two winners means awarding one item to each of two bidders, not two units to one bidder.

= Independent private values model
{parent=Private-value auction}

This model assigns independent private valuation types to the bidders. Symmetry adds identical type distributions and bidder roles. These assumptions determine interim winning probabilities from the valuation distribution in a monotone symmetric equilibrium.

= Symmetric independent private values model
{parent=Independent private values model}

= SIPV model
{synonym}
{c}

The <independent private values model> is symmetric when bidders have identical roles and draw their valuations independently from the same distribution. For <revenue equivalence> in the usual single-object setting, bidders additionally have <risk neutrality> and <quasilinear utility>. Under a strictly increasing symmetric equilibrium bidding rule, a type $v$ wins with probability $F(v)^{n-1}$.

// Target: mathematical-optimization.bigb

= Revenue comparison for two uniform private values
{parent=Independent private values model}

For two independent uniform valuations on $[0,1]$, optimal <posted price> revenue is $2/(3\sqrt3)$, attained at $p=1/\sqrt3$. An <English auction> without reserve earns the expected minimum, $1/3$. With reserve $r\in[0,1]$, revenue is $r\Pr(\text{exactly one valuation exceeds }r)$ plus the expected smaller valuation when both exceed $r$, namely $1/3+r^2-4r^3/3$. It is maximized at $r=1/2$, giving $5/12$. Different reserve-dependent allocation probabilities explain why this differs from the no-reserve revenue without violating <revenue equivalence>.

= Strategyproofness
{parent=Mechanism design}

= Strategyproof
{synonym}

A mechanism or <social choice function> is strategyproof if truthful reporting is a weakly dominant strategy: for every true type and every collection of other reports, no misreport yields strictly higher utility or a strictly preferred outcome. This is stronger than merely requiring truthful reports to form a <Nash equilibrium>.

= Rank-raising monotonicity lemma
{parent=Strategyproofness}

If a <strategyproof> <social choice function> selects $a$, raising $a$ in one agent's order without moving any formerly lower alternative above it preserves the outcome. A different outcome would make one of the changes between the old and new reports a profitable deviation. Arbitrary changes among alternatives wholly above or wholly below $a$ are allowed.

= Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem
{c}
{parent=Strategyproofness}
{wiki=Gibbard–Satterthwaite_theorem}

With a finite set of at least three alternatives and unrestricted strict preference orders, every onto deterministic <strategyproof> <social choice function> is a <dictatorship in social choice>. Restricting to two alternatives or to a restricted preference domain removes essential hypotheses.

= Binary social ordering from strategyproof choice
{parent=Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem}

For an onto <strategyproof> rule on unrestricted strict preference orders, rank-raising monotonicity implies unanimity and respect for unanimous pairwise preference. Promote each alternative pair to the top two positions in all reports. The selected member depends only on each voter's comparison of that pair. These binary choices form a strict transitive social order: a directed three-cycle would contradict the rule's choice when that triple is placed above all other alternatives, because the chosen member must win both of its binary comparisons. The original outcome is the top of this order. This creates a social ordering with pairwise independence without presuming it as an axiom of the original choice rule.

= Two-voter dictatorship from binary choice
{parent=Binary social ordering from strategyproof choice}

For two voters and at least three alternatives, pairwise unanimity, pairwise independence and transitivity force the <binary social ordering from strategyproof choice> to follow one fixed voter. If voter one wins a conflict $a$ against $b$, use profiles $(a>b>c,b>c>a)$ and $(c>a>b,b>c>a)$ to show it also wins $a$ against $c$ and $c$ against $b$. Repeating this implication yields both directions of every pair. If the initial conflict instead follows voter two, exchange the roles. The social choice, being the top of that ordering, is a <dictatorship in social choice>. This proves the two-voter case of the <Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem>.

= Top-bottom decisiveness lemma
{parent=Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem}

For two agents and a <strategyproof> <social choice function>, if an alternative is selected when one agent ranks it first and the other ranks it last, it is selected whenever the first agent ranks it first, whatever the other agent reports. Changing the first agent's order preserves its attainable top choice; changing the second agent's order cannot offer it an improvement over a selected bottom alternative.

= Social choice theory
{parent=Mechanism design}

Social choice theory studies how individual preferences determine a collective decision. A <social choice function> selects one alternative from a preference profile; <strategyproofness> asks whether agents can benefit by misrepresenting their preferences.

= Condorcet winner
{parent=Social choice theory}
{c}

= Strict Condorcet winner
{synonym}

A <Condorcet winner> defeats every other alternative by a strict majority in pairwise voting. It is unique when it exists. For an odd electorate with <single-peaked preferences>, the median peak is a <Condorcet winner>: alternatives on either side lose to the majority whose peaks lie at or beyond the median in the other direction. With an even electorate ties may prevent a strict winner.

= Weak Condorcet winner
{parent=Condorcet winner}

A <weak Condorcet winner> is never defeated by a strict pairwise majority, allowing ties. For even electorates with <single-peaked preferences>, all alternatives between the two middle peaks have this property. A single-valued selection requires a tie convention; arbitrary tie selection is not automatically <strategyproof>.

= Single-peaked preferences
{parent=Social choice theory}
{title2=$a\text{ is a peak on a common linear axis}$}

A strict preference order is single-peaked on an axis when moving away from its most preferred alternative along either one side of the axis makes alternatives less preferred. Comparisons between opposite sides need not follow symmetric physical distance. A profile is single-peaked when all voters' orders share such an axis. This restricted domain admits the <median voter rule>, unlike the unrestricted-domain conclusion of the <Gibbard-Satterthwaite theorem>.

= Median voter rule
{parent=Single-peaked preferences}
{title2=$f(p_1,\ldots,p_n)=\operatorname{median}(p_1,\ldots,p_n)$}

On a fixed common axis, select the median of reported peaks. For odd $n$ this is the unique <Condorcet winner>. For even $n$, a fixed lower-median or upper-median convention selects a <weak Condorcet winner>. Holding other peaks fixed, the attainable outcomes form an interval between two order statistics. Truthful reporting obtains the true peak if it is inside that interval, or its nearest interval endpoint otherwise, so no allowed report improves the outcome under <single-peaked preferences>.

= Social choice function
{parent=Social choice theory}
{title2=$f:\mathcal R^n\to A$}

A deterministic social choice function maps profiles of preference orders to single alternatives. Unrestricted domain permits every profile in $\mathcal R^n$; onto means every alternative lies in the range. A unanimous rule selects an alternative whenever every agent ranks it first.

= Two-alternative majority rule
{parent=Social choice function}

With an odd number of agents and two alternatives, select the alternative ranked first by a strict majority. The rule is onto, <strategyproof>, and for at least three agents has no <dictator in social choice>. A pivotal agent already gets its preferred alternative by reporting truthfully; a nonpivotal agent cannot change the outcome alone.

= Dictatorship in social choice
{parent=Social choice function}
{title2=$f(R)=\operatorname{top}(R_d)$}

A dictatorship always selects the top alternative of one fixed agent $d$. Other agents cannot alter the selection against that agent's preference. This is the social-choice meaning of dictatorship, rather than a political classification.

= Dictator in social choice
{parent=Dictatorship in social choice}
{title2=$d$}

A dictator is the fixed agent whose top-ranked alternative is always selected by a <dictatorship in social choice>.

= Pure strategy
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}
{title2=$s_i\in S_i$}

A <pure strategy> is a definite, complete plan for a player's choices. In a game with one simultaneous choice, it selects one available action with certainty: heads or tails in <matching pennies>, for example. In a sequential game it specifies an action at every situation where that player could be called upon to move, including situations never reached when the plan is followed. The plan can use only information available to the player: indistinguishable decision situations must prescribe the same action. An action at one such situation is only part of the plan.

If $S_i$ is player $i$'s <set> of <pure strategies>, the <payoff> $u_i(s_i,s_{-i})$ depends on the whole <strategy profile>. Thus the same <pure strategy> can be a <best response> against one opposing choice and perform poorly against another. A <dominant strategy> is the stronger case in which it is a <best response> against every opposing <strategy profile>.

= Strict dominance
{parent=Pure strategy}

= Strictly dominated strategy
{synonym}

= Strictly dominated
{synonym}

A strategy is strictly dominated when another strategy gives a strictly larger payoff against every opposing strategy. A strictly dominated strategy cannot receive positive probability in a <Nash equilibrium>, because moving that probability to its dominator increases expected payoff.

= Finite game
{parent=Game theory}

A finite game has finitely many players, each with finitely many <pure strategies>.

= Symmetric finite game
{parent=Finite game}

A <finite game> is symmetric when all players have the same <pure strategies> and permuting the players permutes their payoffs. In the anonymous special case, a player's payoff depends on their own action and the multiset of opposing actions. A <symmetric equilibrium> exists even when there are more than two players, by the <gain-map proof of symmetric equilibrium>.

= Mixed strategy
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}
{title2=$\sigma_i\in\Delta(S_i)$}

A <mixed strategy> is a <probability distribution> over a player's <pure strategies>. For a <finite game> with $m_i$ such strategies, the possible <mixed strategies> form the <probability simplex>
$$
\Delta(S_i)=\left\{\sigma_i\in\mathbb R^{m_i}:\sigma_i(a)\geq0,\quad\sum_{a\in S_i}\sigma_i(a)=1\right\}.
$$
A <pure strategy> is included as a <mixed strategy> placing all <probability> on one choice. The <support of a mixed strategy> consists of the choices with positive <probability>. In a sequential game, sampling a complete <pure strategy> once is the definition; it is not automatically equivalent to fresh randomization at every move.

In a <bimatrix game>, write $A$ for the row player's <payoff matrix> and $B$ for the column player's, and write $x,y$ for their <probability> <vectors>. <Independent> randomization gives <expected payoffs>
$$
U_1(x,y)=x^{\mathsf T}Ay,\qquad U_2(x,y)=x^{\mathsf T}By.
$$
For example, in <matching pennies>, choosing heads with <probabilities> $p=3/4$ and $q=1/4$ gives joint <probabilities> $3/16,9/16,1/16,3/16$ for $(H,H),(H,T),(T,H),(T,T)$. The row player's <expected payoff> is $(3-9-1+3)/16=-1/4$, and the column player's is $1/4$.

Randomization can prevent an opponent exploiting a predictable choice. It does not mean every choice is equally good against every opponent: only <best responses> belong in the <support of a mixed strategy> at a <Nash equilibrium>. A shared random signal can instead produce a <lottery over joint action profiles> whose joint <probabilities> do not factor into <independent> <mixed strategies>.

= Fully mixed strategy
{parent=Mixed strategy}
{title2=$\sigma_i(a)>0\ \text{for every }a\in A_i$}

= Completely mixed strategy
{synonym}

A <mixed strategy> assigning strictly positive <probability> to every available <pure strategy>. Its <support of a mixed strategy> is the entire action <set>. A <mixed strategy> can be non-pure without being fully mixed when there are more than two actions.

= Support of a mixed strategy
{parent=Mixed strategy}
{title2=$\operatorname{supp}(p)=\{i:p_i>0\}$}

= Supports of a mixed strategy
{synonym}

The <support of a mixed strategy> consists of the pure strategies used with positive probability. At a <Nash equilibrium>, every supported pure strategy is a <best response>, while unused strategies may give no higher payoff.

// Target: computer-science.bigb

= Strategy support
{parent=Mixed strategy}
{title2=$\operatorname{supp}(s)=\{i:s_i>0\}$}

The support of a finite <mixed strategy> consists of the <pure strategies> assigned positive probability. At a <Nash equilibrium>, every action in the support is a <best response>. In a game satisfying <nondegeneracy of a bimatrix game>, the two equilibrium supports have equal size.

= Bimatrix game
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

A bimatrix game is a finite two-player game specified by one payoff matrix for each player.

= Matching pennies
{parent=Bimatrix game}
{wiki}

Both players choose heads $H$ or tails $T$ without seeing the other's choice. The row player receives $1$ when the choices match and $-1$ when they differ; the column player receives the opposite <payoff>. Thus <matching pennies> is a <zero-sum game>, with ordered <payoff> pairs:

|| Row choice / column choice
|| $H$
|| $T$

| $H$
| $(1,-1)$
| $(-1,1)$

| $T$
| $(-1,1)$
| $(1,-1)$

At $(H,H)$ the column player improves by switching to $T$; at $(H,T)$ the row player improves by switching to $T$; at $(T,H)$ the row player improves by switching to $H$; at $(T,T)$ the column player improves by switching to $H$. Every <pure-strategy> <strategy profile> therefore has a profitable <unilateral deviation>, so none is a <Nash equilibrium>.

Let $p$ be the row player's <probability> of heads and $q$ the column player's <probability> of heads. Their random choices are <independent>. Against fixed $q$, the row player's pure-choice <expected payoffs> are
$$
U_1(H,q)=2q-1,\qquad U_1(T,q)=1-2q.
$$
Against fixed $p$, the column player's pure-choice <expected payoffs> are
$$
U_2(p,H)=1-2p,\qquad U_2(p,T)=2p-1.
$$
Consequently the <best responses>, expressed as allowed heads <probabilities>, are
$$
\operatorname{BR}_1(q)=\begin{cases}\{0\}&q<1/2,\\ {[0,1]}&q=1/2,\\\{1\}&q>1/2,\end{cases}
\qquad
\operatorname{BR}_2(p)=\begin{cases}\{1\}&p<1/2,\\ {[0,1]}&p=1/2,\\\{0\}&p>1/2.\end{cases}
$$
Here $[0,1]$ means every mixture is a <best response>, including both <pure strategies>. If $q>1/2$, the row player must choose $p=1$, which makes the column player's <best response> $q=0$, a contradiction. If $q<1/2$, the row player must choose $p=0$, which forces $q=1$, also a contradiction. Hence $q=1/2$; for the column player to mix at this $q$, the two column <payoffs> must tie, forcing $p=1/2$. The unique <Nash equilibrium> is therefore
$$
p^*=q^*=\frac12,\qquad U_1(p^*,q^*)=U_2(p^*,q^*)=0.
$$
Indeed, for any $p,q$, $U_1(p,q)=(2p-1)(2q-1)$ and $U_2(p,q)=-U_1(p,q)$. Holding $q=1/2$ gives the row player zero for every deviation $p'$; holding $p=1/2$ gives the column player zero for every deviation $q'$. A player is indifferent against the equilibrium mixture, but choosing an arbitrary <best response> to it need not preserve the opponent's <best response>. This is why that indifference does not produce additional <Nash equilibria>.

\Image[matching-pennies-best-responses.png]
{title=Matching pennies: pure-choice expected payoffs and the unique intersection of best responses}

For a teaching treatment with the same <payoff> convention, see https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/17-810-game-theory-spring-2021/mit17_810s21_lec3.pdf[MIT 17.810, lecture 3, slides 29–38, on mixed strategies and matching pennies].

= Coordination game
{parent=Bimatrix game}
{wiki}

A <coordination game> rewards compatible choices. Players can have a common interest in coordinating while having different preferred coordinated outcomes, so their interests need not be opposite as in a <zero-sum game>.

= Asymmetric coordination example
{parent=Coordination game}
{title2=$(p^*,q^*)=(3/5,2/5)$}

Two players independently choose venues $A$ or $B$. Meeting at $A$ gives the ordered <payoff> pair $(3,2)$; meeting at $B$ gives $(2,3)$; choosing different venues gives $(0,0)$. As throughout this article, the row player's <payoff> comes first:

|| Row choice / column choice
|| $A$
|| $B$

| $A$
| $(3,2)$
| $(0,0)$

| $B$
| $(0,0)$
| $(2,3)$

Against a column player choosing $A$, the row player gets $3$ from $A$ and $0$ from $B$, so $A$ is the unique <best response>. Against $B$, the row player gets $0$ from $A$ and $2$ from $B$, so $B$ is the unique <best response>. The column player likewise prefers to match the row choice. Thus $(A,A)$ and $(B,B)$ are <pure-strategy> <Nash equilibria>, while each mismatched <strategy profile> has a profitable <unilateral deviation>.

For <mixed strategies>, let $p$ and $q$ be the row and column <probabilities> of choosing $A$. The pure-choice <expected payoffs> are
$$
U_1(A,q)=3q,\quad U_1(B,q)=2(1-q),\qquad
U_2(p,A)=2p,\quad U_2(p,B)=3(1-p).
$$
The row player is indifferent when $3q=2(1-q)$, giving $q=2/5$. The column player is indifferent when $2p=3(1-p)$, giving $p=3/5$. The <best responses> are therefore
$$
\operatorname{BR}_1(q)=\begin{cases}\{0\}&q<2/5,\\ {[0,1]}&q=2/5,\\\{1\}&q>2/5,\end{cases}
\qquad
\operatorname{BR}_2(p)=\begin{cases}\{0\}&p<3/5,\\ {[0,1]}&p=3/5,\\\{1\}&p>3/5.\end{cases}
$$
These give exactly three <Nash equilibria>: $(p,q)=(0,0),(1,1),(3/5,2/5)$. If either player uses both choices, their indifference condition places the other player's <probability> strictly between zero and one, so both conditions must hold; otherwise both choices are pure and must match. Notice whose equation determines whose <probability>: the row player's indifference fixes the column player's $q$, and the column player's indifference fixes the row player's $p$.

At the mixed <Nash equilibrium>, <independence> gives
$$
\Pr(A,A)=\frac6{25},\quad \Pr(A,B)=\frac9{25},\quad
\Pr(B,A)=\frac4{25},\quad \Pr(B,B)=\frac6{25}.
$$
Thus they meet with <probability> $12/25$, and each receives <expected payoff> $3(6/25)+2(6/25)=6/5$. Holding $q=2/5$ fixed, every row deviation $p'$ gives $p'(6/5)+(1-p')(6/5)=6/5$; holding $p=3/5$ fixed, every column deviation $q'$ gives $q'(6/5)+(1-q')(6/5)=6/5$. This directly verifies the <Nash equilibrium> inequalities.

Both coordinated <pure-strategy> outcomes strictly improve both players' <expected payoffs> over $(6/5,6/5)$: $(3,2)$ and $(2,3)$ each do so. The mixed <Nash equilibrium> therefore fails <Pareto efficiency>, even though no <unilateral deviation> helps. Switching together can help when switching alone cannot. Neither coordinated outcome improves both players over the other, and both are <Pareto efficient>, even when a <lottery over joint action profiles> is allowed: any positive weight on another outcome reduces the <payoff> of the player receiving $3$.

\Image[coordination-game-equilibria.png]
{title=Asymmetric coordination: three best-response intersections and the cost of independent mixing}

= Lottery over joint action profiles
{parent=Bimatrix game}
{title2=$\rho_{ij}\geq0,\quad\sum_{ij}\rho_{ij}=1$}

= Correlated payoff lottery
{synonym}

A probability distribution over joint action profiles implements a <convex combination> of their two-player payoff vectors. The attainable expected payoffs form the <convex hull> of the pure joint payoffs. The distribution need not factor into independent <mixed strategies>, and a freely enforceable lottery need not satisfy the incentive constraints of a <Nash equilibrium>.

= Support enumeration for a bimatrix game
{parent=Bimatrix game}

Enumerate pairs of <strategy supports>, solve the equations making each supported action indifferent, then check strict positivity of supported probabilities and all omitted-action payoff inequalities. These conditions suffice for a <Nash equilibrium>. Under <nondegeneracy of a bimatrix game>, only pairs of equal support size need be tested.

= Symmetric bimatrix game
{title2=$Q=P^T$}
{parent=Bimatrix game}

A square <bimatrix game> is symmetric when exchanging players exchanges their payoffs: its <payoff matrices> are $P,Q$ with $Q=P^T$. If $(x,y)$ is a <Nash equilibrium>, then $(y,x)$ is also a <Nash equilibrium>. A <symmetric bimatrix game> can still have asymmetric equilibria, so the two strategy vectors must be kept separate in the <Lemke-Howson algorithm>.

= Nondegeneracy of a bimatrix game
{parent=Bimatrix game}

A <bimatrix game> is nondegenerate if every <mixed strategy> with $k$ positive coordinates has at most $k$ pure <best responses> for the other player. This condition prevents ties in the leaving-variable choice along the usual <Lemke-Howson algorithm> path and allows unique continuation once the dropped label has been selected.

= Nash equilibrium
{c}
{parent=Bimatrix game}
{wiki}
{title2=$\sigma^*$}

= Nash equilibria
{c}
{synonym}

A <Nash equilibrium> is a <strategy profile> in which every player's strategy is a <best response> to the others. For a <finite game> and <mixed strategies>, its precise condition is
$$
U_i(\sigma_i^*,\sigma_{-i}^*)\geq U_i(\tau_i,\sigma_{-i}^*)
\quad\text{for every player }i\text{ and every }\tau_i\in\Delta(S_i).
$$
The opponents' strategies $\sigma_{-i}^*$ are held fixed while testing each <unilateral deviation>. Equality is allowed: a player may have other equally good <best responses>. Because <expected payoff> is a <linear function> of the player's own mixture, it suffices to test every <pure strategy>. Equivalently, every choice in the <support of a mixed strategy> must attain the largest pure-choice <expected payoff>, and no unused choice may exceed it.

The two worked examples illustrate different uses of this condition. <Matching pennies> has no <pure-strategy> <Nash equilibrium>, but its unique <mixed-strategy> <Nash equilibrium> removes each player's vulnerability to predictable play. The <asymmetric coordination example> has three <Nash equilibria>, and its mixed one fails <Pareto efficiency>. A <Nash equilibrium> tests individual incentives, whereas <Pareto efficiency> compares feasible outcomes that may require several players to change together. In the <prisoner's dilemma>, for example, mutual defection is a <Nash equilibrium> even though mutual cooperation gives both players a larger <payoff> because $R>P$.

Existence in a <finite game> is guaranteed by <Nash's theorem>. The theorem does not select one <Nash equilibrium>, promise <Pareto efficiency>, or assert convergence of a learning process. Nash's https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1063129/[1950 existence paper]{ref} used the <Kakutani fixed-point theorem>; his https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~klarson/teaching/F04-886/papers/nash51.pdf[1951 paper, section Existence of Equilibrium Points]{ref} gave a proof using the <Brouwer fixed-point theorem>. The connection to a <fixed point> captures the idea of choices that remain mutually optimal when considered together.

= Fully mixed Nash equilibrium
{parent=Nash equilibrium}

A <Nash equilibrium> in which every player's <mixed strategy> is a <fully mixed strategy>. Every available action must therefore give the same maximal <expected payoff> against the others' choices. These indifference equations, together with strictly positive <probabilities> summing to one, suffice to verify such a <Nash equilibrium>.

= Existential equilibrium checking in a Boolean game
{parent=Nash equilibrium}

Given a <Boolean game> and a query <propositional formula> $\phi$, decide whether some <pure strategy> <Nash equilibrium> satisfies $\phi$. The problem is complete for the existential <second level of the polynomial hierarchy>.

For membership, existentially choose a <Boolean valuation> and universally check every player's every <unilateral deviation>. The goal must remain false under all such deviations for each player whose goal is currently false. Each individual check takes <polynomial time>.

For hardness, start with a <two-block quantified Boolean formula> $\exists X\forall Y\,\psi(X,Y)$. Give player 1 control of $X$ and the constant true goal. Give player 2 control of $Y$ and goal $\neg\psi$, and use $\psi$ as the query. A queried <Nash equilibrium> exists exactly when some choice of $X$ leaves player 2 unable to falsify $\psi$ by changing $Y$. This is precisely the input quantified statement, and the construction is a <polynomial-time reduction>.

= Ranked university application game
{parent=Nash equilibrium}

There are $mq$ distinctly ranked students and $m$ labelled universities with $q$ seats each. Each student applies to one university; each university selects the greatest ranks, and admitted students receive their admitted cohort's average rank. At a pure <Nash equilibrium>, every student is admitted: any rejected student could move to a university with a vacancy. The university with the largest average must contain the top $q$ ranks, since any omitted higher rank could displace its lowest rank and improve. Repeat on the remaining students to obtain consecutive rank blocks. Assigning these blocks to the labelled universities gives exactly $m!$ pure equilibria.

= Symmetric Nash equilibrium
{parent=Nash equilibrium}

In a game invariant under permuting players, a symmetric <Nash equilibrium> gives every player the same strategy, which may be a <mixed strategy>. Independent randomization does not require realized resource loads to be equal. When resource delays are affine, a player's expected delay depends on their own certain resource use plus the expected use by the other players; replacing all loads by unconditional total expected loads loses that own-player correction.

= Equilibrium oddness theorem
{parent=Nash equilibrium}

A finite nondegenerate <bimatrix game> has a finite odd number of <Nash equilibria>. After shifting to positive payoffs, fully labelled vertex pairs in the bounded best-response polytopes correspond to equilibria plus one artificial zero pair. Dropping a fixed label gives a finite path graph whose endpoints are exactly these pairs. The number of endpoints is even, leaving an odd number of genuine equilibria. This is the endpoint-parity argument underlying the <Lemke-Howson algorithm>.

= Symmetric equilibrium
{parent=Nash equilibrium}
{wiki}

= Symmetric equilibria
{synonym}

A symmetric equilibrium of a symmetric game has every player use the same strategy or the same distribution of strategies. Symmetry between players does not require identical behavior across contests with different prizes.

= Symmetric equilibrium parity
{parent=Symmetric equilibrium}

In a nondegenerate <symmetric bimatrix game>, player interchange pairs every nonsymmetric <Nash equilibrium> with a distinct swapped equilibrium. The <equilibrium oddness theorem> gives an odd total; the remaining fixed points, which are the <symmetric equilibria>, therefore also number an odd amount.

= Symmetric Nash gain map
{parent=Symmetric equilibrium}
{title2=$T_i(x)=\frac{x_i+[(Px)_i-x^TPx]_+}{1+\sum_j[(Px)_j-x^TPx]_+}$}

The continuous gain map takes a <probability simplex> to itself. The <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> supplies a fixed point, whose positive gains would contradict the zero strategy-weighted average payoff excess. Thus all gains vanish, giving a strategy that is a <best response> to itself. In a <symmetric bimatrix game>, this produces a <symmetric equilibrium>.

= Gain-map proof of symmetric equilibrium
{parent=Symmetric Nash gain map}
{title2=$F_i(p)=\dfrac{p_i+[e(i,p)-e(p)]_+}{1+\sum_j[e(j,p)-e(p)]_+}$}

For a <symmetric finite game>, pure-action expected payoffs $e(i,p)$ against independent identical opposing <mixed strategies> are continuous. The displayed map takes the <probability simplex> continuously into itself. At a <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> fixed point, put $r_i=[e(i,p)-e(p)]_+$ and $S=\sum_i r_i$. The fixed-point equations give $r_i=p_iS$. If $S>0$, every supported action has strictly positive excess payoff, contradicting $\sum_i p_i(e(i,p)-e(p))=0$. Hence $S=0$ and every supported action is a <best response>, proving a <symmetric Nash equilibrium>.

= Complementarity construction of a symmetric Nash equilibrium
{parent=Nash equilibrium}
{title2=$Px+z=\mathbf1,\quad x,z\geq0,\quad x^{\mathsf T}z=0$}

For a symmetric <bimatrix game> with payoff matrices $P,P^{\mathsf T}$, any nonzero $x$ satisfying these equations gives the symmetric <Nash equilibrium> $(x/\sum_i x_i,x/\sum_i x_i)$. Every positive coordinate attains the same maximal payoff. This one-vector construction differs from a two-vector <Lemke-Howson algorithm> path, which can return asymmetric equilibria.

= Lemke-Howson algorithm
{c}
{parent=Nash equilibrium}
{wiki=Lemke–Howson_algorithm}

The Lemke-Howson algorithm follows an almost completely labeled path in the product of two best-response polytopes of a <bimatrix game>. Drop one label at the artificial zero pair, then alternately pivot away the duplicated label until the dropped label returns. A nonzero completely labeled endpoint, after normalization, gives a <Nash equilibrium>. The two players' vectors must be kept separate even in a symmetric game.

= Complementary pivoting
{title2=$x_ir_i=0,\quad y_js_j=0$}
{parent=Lemke-Howson algorithm}

Complementary pivoting follows adjacent bases while maintaining all but one label of a complementarity system. The <Lemke-Howson algorithm> for a <bimatrix game> follows the duplicated label between two tableaux until it restores the dropped label at a nonzero completely labelled pair. Normalization then gives a <Nash equilibrium>.

= Nash's theorem
{c}
{parent=Nash equilibrium}

Nash's theorem states that every <finite game> has at least one <Nash equilibrium> in <mixed strategies>.

= Brouwer gain-map proof of bimatrix equilibrium
{c}
{parent=Nash's theorem}
{title2=$T_i(p,q)=\frac{p_i+[(Aq)_i-p^TAq]_+}{1+\sum_j[(Aq)_j-p^TAq]_+}$}

For each player in a finite <bimatrix game>, add the positive pure-strategy gains to its current <mixed strategy> and normalize. This gives a continuous self-map of the product of <probability simplices>. At a <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> fixed point, the gain coordinates equal the current strategy times their total gain. If that total were positive, every supported action would beat the current average payoff, contradicting that average. Thus every pure deviation has nonpositive gain for both players, proving a <Nash equilibrium>.

= Brouwer proof of Nash equilibrium for a two-by-two game
{parent=Nash equilibrium}

Normalize each player's positive pure-strategy payoff improvements to define a continuous self-map of the product of their mixed-strategy simplices. At a Brouwer fixed point every positive improvement must vanish, giving a Nash equilibrium.

= Best response
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}
{title2=$\operatorname{BR}_i(\sigma_{-i})$}

= Best response correspondence
{synonym}

A <best response> maximizes a player's <expected payoff> while the other players' strategies are held fixed. In a <finite game>, write the full <set> of <mixed-strategy> <best responses> as
$$
\operatorname{BR}_i(\sigma_{-i})=
\left\{\tau_i\in\Delta(S_i):U_i(\tau_i,\sigma_{-i})=
\max_{\rho_i\in\Delta(S_i)}U_i(\rho_i,\sigma_{-i})\right\}.
$$
This is generally a <set-valued mapping>, since several choices may tie. Every mixture supported on maximizing <pure strategies> is a <best response>, and mixing in a strictly worse choice reduces <expected payoff>.

For example, against $q=3/4$ in <matching pennies>, the row player's heads and tails <expected payoffs> are $1/2$ and $-1/2$, so heads is the unique <best response>. Against $q=1/2$, all mixtures are <best responses>. In the <asymmetric coordination example>, against $q=3/4$ the row player gets $9/4$ from $A$ and $1/2$ from $B$, so $A$ is optimal. A <Nash equilibrium> requires everyone to be a <best response> simultaneously; one optimal reply alone does not establish it.

= Dominant strategy
{parent=Best response}
{wiki}

A dominant strategy is a <best response> for every strategy profile of the other players.

= Weakly dominant strategy
{parent=Dominant strategy}

A <strategy> giving at least as large a <payoff> as every alternative against every opponent <strategy profile>. Under the convention distinguishing weak dominance from complete payoff equivalence, require a strict advantage somewhere. In a two-action <finite game>, it suffices to check the opponent's <pure strategies>: <expected payoff> differences against an opponent's <mixed strategy> are weighted averages of those pure-action comparisons.

= Dominant-strategy equilibrium
{parent=Dominant strategy}
{title2=$s_i\in\bigcap_{s_{-i}}\operatorname{BR}_i(s_{-i})\quad\forall i$}

A <strategy profile> in which each player uses a <dominant strategy>. Every such profile is a <Nash equilibrium>, since each prescribed choice is a <best response> to the actual opposing choices in particular. The converse fails: a <Nash equilibrium> only requires a <best response> to those actual choices. With the weak convention for <dominant strategy>, ties are allowed; a <strictly dominant strategy> requires strict improvement over every alternative against every opposing profile.

= Strictly dominant strategy
{parent=Dominant strategy}

A strictly dominant strategy gives a strictly greater payoff than every alternative strategy for every strategy profile of the other players.

= Zero-sum game
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}
{title2=$\sum_i u_i(s)=0$}

In a <zero-sum game>, the players' <payoffs> sum to zero at every <pure-strategy> <strategy profile>. For two players this means $u_2=-u_1$: maximizing the column player's <payoff> is minimizing the row player's. A single <payoff matrix> $A$ therefore specifies a finite two-player <matrix game>, and <independent> <mixed strategies> $x,y$ give <expected payoffs> $x^{\mathsf T}Ay$ and $-x^{\mathsf T}Ay$.

<Matching pennies> and the usual <rock paper scissors> are two-player <zero-sum games>. The <asymmetric coordination example> is not: its total <payoff> is $5$ when choices match and $0$ otherwise. Applying a two-player <minimax theorem> to general <bimatrix games> would discard this possibility of shared gains. For a two-player <zero-sum game>, however, <optimal mixed strategies> guarantee the <value of a zero-sum game>, and pairing any two opposing optimal strategies gives a <Nash equilibrium>.

= Constant-sum game
{parent=Zero-sum game}
{title2=$u_1+u_2=K$}

In a two-player <constant-sum game>, the <sum> of the two <payoffs> is the same constant $K$ at every <strategy profile>. Replacing each player's <payoff> by $u_i-K/2$ gives a <zero-sum game> without changing <best responses> or <Nash equilibria>. If both players can guarantee $K/2$, then any <Nash equilibrium> gives each that value. An equilibrium in <pure strategies> must use <pure strategies> with that guarantee: otherwise the opponent could improve on $K/2$ by a <unilateral deviation>.

= Rock paper scissors
{parent=Zero-sum game}
{wiki}

With choices ordered rock, scissors, paper and winner payoff $1$, the row-player payoff matrix is $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&-1\\-1&0&1\\1&-1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Both the row and column sums vanish. Thus the uniform <mixed strategy> guarantees expected payoff zero against every opposing strategy; the <value of a zero-sum game> is zero. A player responding to a known nonuniform mixture can instead optimize its linear expected payoff over pure responses and their ties.

= Value of a zero-sum game
{parent=Zero-sum game}
{title2=$v=\max_r\min_s r^{\mathsf T}As=\min_s\max_r r^{\mathsf T}As$}

For a finite matrix <zero-sum game>, the value is the payoff that the maximizing player can guarantee and the minimizing player can prevent the payoff from exceeding, using <mixed strategies>. The common value of the two displayed optimizations exists by the <minimax theorem>. A pair of strategies can be verified directly by checking that every pure column pays the row mixture at least $v$ and every pure row earns at most $v$ against the column mixture.

= Cost-weighted finite search game
{parent=Zero-sum game}
{title2=$v=(c^2+\sum_i c_i^2)/(2c)$}

In a hide-and-search <zero-sum game> with positive inspection costs $c_i$ and $c=\sum_i c_i$, hiding with probabilities $c_i/c$ gives the same expected search cost for every complete search order. For three locations, beginning at $i$ with probability $c_i/c$ and randomizing the remaining two orders also equalizes every hiding location. The common value is the displayed quantity.

= Matrix game
{parent=Zero-sum game}

A matrix game is a finite two-player zero-sum game whose entry $A_{ij}$ is the row player's payoff when the players choose row $i$ and column $j$.

= Positive-payoff linear programming for a matrix game
{parent=Matrix game}
{title2=$\min\mathbf1^Tx:\ B^Tx\ge\mathbf1,\ x\ge0$}

When every entry of a <payoff matrix> $B$ is positive, its game value $v$ is positive. Setting $x=p/v$ converts the row player's <probability> vector and guaranteed value into the displayed <linear program>, whose optimum is $1/v$. Conversely normalizing a feasible $x$ gives a <mixed strategy> guaranteeing $1/(\mathbf1^Tx)$. The dual maximizes $\mathbf1^Ty$ under $By\le\mathbf1$, $y\ge0$ and recovers the column strategy by normalization. Adding a constant to every payoff preserves <Nash equilibria> and lets this positive-payoff formulation handle general finite <zero-sum games>.

= Payoff matrix
{parent=Matrix game}
{title2=$A=(a_{ij})$}

A payoff matrix records the row player's gains when row $i$ meets column $j$. In a <zero-sum game> the other player's payoff is the negative. For <mixed strategies> $p,q$, the expected row payoff is $p^TAq$.

= Matrix-game optimization problem
{parent=Matrix game}

The row player solves
$$
\max_{p,v}v
\quad\text{subject to}\quad
A^Tp\geq ve,quad e^Tp=1,quad p\geq0.
$$

= Mixed-strategy optimality certificate for a matrix game
{parent=Matrix game}

If the column player has an optimal strategy at value $v$ and a row probability vector $p$ satisfies $p^TA\geq ve^T$, then $p$ is optimal: it guarantees $v$, while the optimal column strategy prevents every row strategy from exceeding $v$.

= Symmetric inverse formula for a matrix-game equilibrium
{parent=Matrix game}

If a symmetric invertible payoff matrix satisfies $A^{-1}e\geq0$, both players may use
$$
p=q=\frac{A^{-1}e}{e^TA^{-1}e},
$$
and the game value is $(e^TA^{-1}e)^{-1}$.

= Three-card threshold-sum zero-sum game
{parent=Symmetric inverse formula for a matrix-game equilibrium}

For
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}2&3&4\\3&4&-5\\4&-5&-6\end{pmatrix},
$$
both players optimally choose cards $1,2,3$ with probabilities $41/50,2/25,1/10$, and the row player's expected payoff is $57/25$.

= Minimax theorem
{parent=Zero-sum game}
{wiki}
{title2=$\max_x\min_y x^{\mathsf T}Ay=\min_y\max_x x^{\mathsf T}Ay$}

For every real $m\times n$ <payoff matrix> $A$ of a two-player <zero-sum game>, the <minimax theorem> states
$$
\max_{x\in\Delta_m}\min_{y\in\Delta_n}x^{\mathsf T}Ay
=\min_{y\in\Delta_n}\max_{x\in\Delta_m}x^{\mathsf T}Ay=v,
$$
where $\Delta_m,\Delta_n$ are the players' <probability simplices>. Both outer optima are attained. The row player's <security level payoff> is the largest <payoff> it can guarantee against all opposing choices; the column player can hold that <payoff> to the same number $v$, the <value of a zero-sum game>. For optimal $x^*,y^*$,
$$
x^{\mathsf T}Ay^*\leq v=(x^*)^{\mathsf T}Ay^*\leq(x^*)^{\mathsf T}Ay
\quad\text{for all }x\in\Delta_m,\ y\in\Delta_n.
$$
These are exactly the <Nash equilibrium> inequalities under the row-maximizes, column-minimizes convention. Conversely, a <Nash equilibrium> of this two-player <zero-sum game> supplies both guarantees. This equality explains why independent worst-case <optimization> solves the opposing incentives in this special class.

Allowing <mixed strategies> is essential. For <matching pennies>, $A=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$. Restricting both players to <pure strategies> gives $\max_i\min_j A_{ij}=-1$ and $\min_j\max_i A_{ij}=1$. Allowing <mixed strategies> closes the gap: each fair mixture guarantees zero, so $v=0$. These guarantees hold even if the opponent knows the mixture's <probabilities>, provided the opponent does not observe the sampled choice before choosing.

John von Neumann proved the two-player <zero-sum game> result in https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01448847[Zur Theorie der Gesellschaftsspiele, Mathematische Annalen 100, 295–320 (1928)]; the original article's footnote 7 explicitly discusses the equality of max–min and min–max. Nash's https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1063129/[1950 paper]{ref} states that existence of a <Nash equilibrium> is equivalent to this result in the two-player <zero-sum game> case, and extends existence to all <finite games>.

= Optimal mixed strategy
{parent=Zero-sum game}
{wiki}

An optimal mixed strategy guarantees the value of the game against every opposing pure strategy.

= Antisymmetric zero-sum game
{parent=Zero-sum game}
{wiki}

A square game with antisymmetric payoff matrix has value zero.

= Support certificate for an antisymmetric matrix game
{parent=Antisymmetric zero-sum game}
{title2=$Ap\le0,\quad p\ge0,\quad\mathbf1^Tp=1$}

For an antisymmetric <payoff matrix>, such a vector guarantees that an opponent playing rows cannot win positively against it. Antisymmetry also gives $p^TA\ge0$, so the same <mixed strategy> guarantees nonnegative payoff as a row strategy and proves game value zero. Furthermore $p^TAp=0$ forces $(Ap)_i=0$ whenever $p_i>0$. To prove uniqueness, use one certified strategy $q$: any other optimal $p$ satisfies $q^TAp=-(Aq)^Tp$, so a strict negative component of $Aq$ forces the corresponding probability in $p$ to vanish.

= Consecutive-number antisymmetric game
{parent=Antisymmetric zero-sum game}

In this game the higher of two chosen integers wins one unit if they are consecutive, but pays two units if their difference is at least two. Ties pay zero. For any choices $1,\ldots,n$ with $n\ge3$, both players can optimally choose $1,2,3$ with probabilities $1/4,1/2,1/4$ and assign zero probability to larger numbers. If $p$ is this vector, $Ap$ is zero in its first three entries, $-5/4$ in its fourth when present, and $-2$ in all later entries. Thus $Ap\le0$ and $p^TA\ge0$, proving the zero value and both security guarantees.

= Dominated strategy
{parent=Game theory}
{wiki}

A dominated strategy can be replaced by another strategy that performs at least as well against every opponent action.

= Very weakly dominated strategy
{parent=Dominated strategy}
{title2=$u_i(s_i,s_{-i})\leq u_i(\sigma_i,s_{-i})\text{ for every }s_{-i}$}

= Very weakly dominated
{synonym}

= Very weak dominance
{synonym}

A <pure strategy> is <very weakly dominated> by another strategy when its <payoff> is no greater against every opposing pure <strategy profile>, with no strict advantage required anywhere. The definition permits <payoff-equivalent strategies> and even domination by the original strategy itself. A <weakly dominated strategy> instead has a strict inequality for at least one opposing <strategy profile>.

In a <binary game>, suppose $\sigma_i$ very weakly dominates $s_i$ and assigns positive <probability> to a different pure action. At every opposing <strategy profile> where $s_i$ pays one, all actions in the <support of a mixed strategy> must also pay one. Where $s_i$ pays zero, their <payoffs> are automatically no smaller. Therefore every distinct supported action very weakly dominates $s_i$. If the mixed domination is weak with a strict improvement somewhere, choose a supported action paying one at a profile where $s_i$ pays zero; that action also supplies a strict improvement. The requirement of nontrivial support is indispensable for the very-weak version.

= Dominated strategy elimination
{parent=Dominated strategy}

In a finite <zero-sum game>, removing a <strictly dominated strategy> preserves the <value of a zero-sum game>: replace any probability on it by the dominating <pure strategy>. This increases every payoff for the maximizing player, or decreases every payoff for the minimizing player. Repeat on the reduced game; new domination relations may appear after a deletion.

= Weakly dominated strategy
{parent=Dominated strategy}

= Weakly dominated
{synonym}

A strategy is weakly dominated if another gives at least as much payoff against every opponent strategy and strictly more against at least one. Unlike a <strictly dominated strategy>, it may remain a <best response> to opponents who never choose a strategy where the comparison is strict. Thus <weak domination does not exclude equilibrium strategies>.

= Equilibrium preservation under iterated weak dominance
{parent=Weakly dominated strategy}

Starting from the full finite <bimatrix game>, sequentially remove actions weakly dominated by current <mixed strategies>, with a strict improvement somewhere. At least one <Nash equilibrium> survives on the final action sets. Take an <Nash equilibrium> of the reduced game and restore actions in reverse. A restored action is no better against the fixed surviving opponent strategy than its dominating mixture of already available actions, so it introduces no profitable deviation. Any self-weight in a dominating mixture can be removed and renormalized because strict improvement forces that weight below one. Using dominance tested only against an arbitrary initially restricted opponent set does not guarantee an <Nash equilibrium> of the original full game.

= Weak domination does not exclude equilibrium strategies
{parent=Weakly dominated strategy}

In the <zero-sum game> with row payoff matrix $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\0&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, the second row is weakly dominated by the first. Nevertheless second row and first column form a <Nash equilibrium>: both rows tie against that column, and both columns tie against that row. Weak dominance therefore cannot be used to discard all possible equilibrium strategies without further qualifications.
